window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-aod","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Application of Derivatives · Practice","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are decreasing on the interval $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$?\n$\\cos x$, $\\cos 2x$, $\\cos 3x$, $\\tan x$, $\\log \\sin x$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take $x \\in \\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ and check the sign of each derivative.\n(i) $\\cos x$: the derivative is $-\\sin x < 0$, so it is decreasing.\n(ii) $\\cos 2x$: the derivative is $-2\\sin 2x$. Here $2x \\in \\left(0, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$, so $\\sin 2x > 0$ and the function is decreasing.\n(iii) $\\cos 3x$: the derivative is $-3\\sin 3x$. Here $3x \\in (0, \\pi)$, so $\\sin 3x > 0$ and the function is decreasing.\n(iv) $\\tan x$: the derivative is $\\sec^2 x > 0$, so it is increasing.\n(v) $\\log \\sin x$: the derivative is $\\cot x > 0$, so it is increasing.\nThree functions are decreasing. Answer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"An open box is made from a square tin sheet of side 30 cm. Equal squares are cut from each corner and the flaps are folded up. What is the maximum possible volume of the box?","image":"","options":["2000 cm$^3$","1800 cm$^3$","2250 cm$^3$","1000 cm$^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the cut square have side $x$. Then $V=x(30-2x)^2=4x^3-120x^2+900x$.\n$V'=12x^2-240x+900=12(x-5)(x-15)=0$, so $x=5$ or $x=15$.\nReject $x=15$, since it gives no base at all.\n$V''=24x-240$, so $V''(5)=-120<0$, which confirms a maximum.\nSubstituting: $V(5)=5\\times20^2=2000$ cm$^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x + 5$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nStatement I: f is increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nStatement II: $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for all real x, and $f'(x) = 0$ only at the single point $x = 1$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 6x + 3 = 3(x-1)^2$.\nStatement II: $3(x-1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all x, and it equals 0 only at $x = 1$. So II is true.\nStatement I: f' never changes sign and vanishes at only one isolated point. So f is increasing on $[1, \\infty)$ and on $(-\\infty, 1]$, and hence on $\\mathbb{R}$. So I is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x^3 - 12x$ on the closed interval $[-3, 5]$, what is the value of (absolute maximum value) $-$ (absolute minimum value)?","image":"","options":["32","81","49","25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Critical points: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12 = 0$ gives $x = \\pm 2$. Both lie in $[-3, 5]$.\nValues at the critical points and end points:\n$f(-3) = 9$, $f(-2) = 16$, $f(2) = -16$, $f(5) = 65$.\nAbsolute maximum $= 65$ (at the end point $x = 5$). Absolute minimum $= -16$.\nDifference $= 65 - (-16) = 81$.\nThe value 32 uses the local maximum 16 instead of the end-point value 65.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = \\frac{4\\sin x - 2x - x\\cos x}{2 + \\cos x}$, $x \\in (0, 2\\pi)$. Then f is decreasing on","image":"","options":["$(0, \\pi)$","$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$","$(\\pi, 2\\pi)$","$\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\cup \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Simplify first. Since $-2x - x\\cos x = -x(2 + \\cos x)$, we get $f(x) = \\frac{4\\sin x}{2 + \\cos x} - x$.\nBy the quotient rule, $f'(x) = \\frac{4\\cos x(2 + \\cos x) + 4\\sin^2 x}{(2 + \\cos x)^2} - 1 = \\frac{8\\cos x + 4}{(2 + \\cos x)^2} - 1$.\nOver a common denominator: $f'(x) = \\frac{8\\cos x + 4 - (4 + 4\\cos x + \\cos^2 x)}{(2 + \\cos x)^2} = \\frac{\\cos x(4 - \\cos x)}{(2 + \\cos x)^2}$.\nBoth $4 - \\cos x > 0$ and $(2 + \\cos x)^2 > 0$, so the sign of $f'(x)$ is the sign of $\\cos x$.\n$\\cos x < 0$ on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$, so f is decreasing there. Option (4) is where f is increasing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given by $x = t^2 + 1$, $y = t^3 - 12t$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3t^2 - 12}{2t}$ for $t \\neq 0$\n(ii) $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$ at t = 2 and at t = -2\n(iii) At t = 1, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{9}{2}$\n(iv) The formula $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$ cannot be used at t = 0","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First find $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2 - 12$.\n(i) Correct: the chain rule gives $\\frac{3t^2 - 12}{2t}$, which is valid for $t \\neq 0$.\n(ii) Correct: $3t^2 - 12 = 0$ gives $t = \\pm 2$, and $2t \\neq 0$ there.\n(iii) Incorrect: at t = 1, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3 - 12}{2} = -\\frac{9}{2}$.\n(iv) Correct: at t = 0, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 0$, so the formula does not apply.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A point on the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is at distances 8 cm and 1 cm from the two perpendicular sides. What is the minimum possible length of the hypotenuse?","image":"","options":["$5\\sqrt5$ cm","9 cm","$\\sqrt{65}$ cm","$3\\sqrt3$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the hypotenuse make angle $\\theta$ with the side that is 8 cm from the point. Then $L=8\\csc\\theta+\\sec\\theta$, with $0<\\theta<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n$L'=-8\\csc\\theta\\cot\\theta+\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta=0$ gives $\\tan^3\\theta=8$, so $\\tan\\theta=2$.\n$L'$ changes from − to + at this point, so it is a minimum.\nHere $\\sin\\theta=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$ and $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$.\nSo $L=4\\sqrt5+\\sqrt5=5\\sqrt5\\approx11.18$ cm.\nThis agrees with the formula $(a^{2/3}+b^{2/3})^{3/2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The side of a square metal plate increases at the rate of 0.5 cm/s when it is heated. At what rate is its area increasing when the side is 8 cm?","image":"","options":["8 cm$^2$/s","4 cm$^2$/s","16 cm$^2$/s","64 cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 0.5$ cm/s and $x = 8$ cm.\nFormula: $A = x^2$, so by the chain rule $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2x\\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2(8)(0.5) = 8$.\nSo the area increases at 8 cm$^2$/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 3x + 7$ is increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) = 3(x+1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all real x, and $f'(x) = 0$ only at the single point $x = -1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $f'(x) = 3x^2 + 6x + 3 = 3(x+1)^2$. This is never negative and is zero only at $x = -1$. So R is true.\nA: f' is positive on $(-\\infty, -1)$ and on $(-1, \\infty)$, and f is continuous at $-1$. Hence f is increasing on each piece and therefore on all of $\\mathbb{R}$. So A is true.\nThe non-negative derivative, vanishing at an isolated point, is exactly why f increases. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = \\sin x + \\cos x$ is decreasing on the interval $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)$.\nReason (R): For every $x \\in \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)$, $\\sin x > \\cos x$, so $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = \\cos x - \\sin x$, which is zero at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $x = \\frac{5\\pi}{4}$ in $[0, 2\\pi]$.\nBetween these points, $\\sin x > \\cos x$ (for example, at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $1 > 0$). So $f'(x) < 0$ and R is true.\nA negative derivative throughout the interval means $f$ is decreasing there, so A is true.\nR is exactly the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + 3$ is strictly increasing on which interval?","image":"","options":["$(-\\infty, 2)$","$(2, \\infty)$","$(-\\infty, -2)$","$(-2, \\infty)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 4x - 8 = 4(x-2)$.\nSet $f'(x) = 0$ to get the critical point $x = 2$. It splits the real line into $(-\\infty, 2)$ and $(2, \\infty)$.\nFor $x > 2$, $f'(x) > 0$, so f is increasing there. For $x < 2$, $f'(x) < 0$, so f is decreasing there.\nThe tempting option $(-2, \\infty)$ uses the wrong sign of the critical point.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = \\log|\\cos x|$ is increasing on $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi\\right)$.\nReason (R): On $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi\\right)$, $f'(x) = -\\tan x$ and $\\tan x < 0$ there.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"On $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi\\right)$, $\\cos x > 0$, so $f(x) = \\log \\cos x$.\nThen $f'(x) = \\frac{-\\sin x}{\\cos x} = -\\tan x$.\nIn the fourth quadrant $\\sin x < 0$ and $\\cos x > 0$, so $\\tan x < 0$. Hence $f'(x) = -\\tan x > 0$, and R is true.\nA positive derivative on the interval makes f increasing, so A is true and R explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x + 15$, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$x = 3$ is a point of local maxima and the local maximum value is 15","$x = 1$ is a point of local maxima and the local maximum value is 19","$x = 1$ is a point of local minima and the local minimum value is 19","$x = 3$ is a point of local minima and the local minimum value is 19"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12x + 9 = 3(x-1)(x-3)$, so the critical points are $x = 1$ and $x = 3$.\n$f''(x) = 6x - 12$.\nAt $x = 1$: $f''(1) = -6 < 0$, so $x = 1$ is a local maximum with $f(1) = 1 - 6 + 9 + 15 = 19$.\nAt $x = 3$: $f''(3) = 6 > 0$, so $x = 3$ is a local minimum with $f(3) = 27 - 54 + 27 + 15 = 15$.\nOnly option (2) matches both the nature and the value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = 2x^3 - 9x^2 + 12x + 5$, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$f$ is decreasing in $(1, 2)$","$f$ is increasing in $(0, 2)$","The local maximum value of $f$ is $10$","The local minimum value of $f$ is $9$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 6x^2 - 18x + 12 = 6(x-1)(x-2)$.\nSign of $f'$: positive on $(-\\infty, 1)$, negative on $(1, 2)$, positive on $(2, \\infty)$. So option (1) is correct.\nAt $x = 1$, $f'$ changes from $+$ to $-$, giving a local maximum: $f(1) = 2 - 9 + 12 + 5 = 10$. Option (3) is correct.\nAt $x = 2$, $f'$ changes from $-$ to $+$, giving a local minimum: $f(2) = 16 - 36 + 24 + 5 = 9$. Option (4) is correct.\nOption (2) is incorrect because $f$ decreases on $(1, 2)$, which lies inside $(0, 2)$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = |x|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nAssertion (A): $x = 0$ is a critical point of $f$.\nReason (R): A point $c$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point if either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f$ is not differentiable at $c$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. It is the NCERT definition of a critical point.\nA is true. The graph of $|x|$ has a corner at 0, so $f'(0)$ does not exist. That is exactly the second condition in R, so 0 qualifies as a critical point.\nA holds precisely because of R, so R correctly explains A. (Here $f'(0)$ is not zero; it simply does not exist.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $f(x) = x^3$, the point $x = 0$ is neither a point of local maxima nor a point of local minima, even though $f'(0) = 0$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) = 3x^2$ does not change sign as $x$ increases through 0.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $f'(0)=0$, yet $f(x)<0$ for $x<0$ and $f(x)>0$ for $x>0$, so 0 is not an extremum. This shows the converse of Theorem 2 fails.\nR is true: $3x^2>0$ on both sides of 0, so the sign of $f'$ does not change.\nBy the first derivative test, no sign change means no local extremum; such a point is a point of inflexion. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The local minimum value of $g(x) = \\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{9}{x}$, $x > 0$, is","image":"","options":["3","6","1.5","4.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First derivative: $g'(x)=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{9}{x^2}$.\nSetting $g'(x)=0$ gives $x^2=36$. Since $x>0$, $x=6$.\nSecond derivative: $g''(x)=\\frac{18}{x^3}$, so $g''(6)=\\frac{18}{216}>0$. Hence $x=6$ is a point of local minima.\nLocal minimum value: $g(6)=\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{9}{6}=1.5+1.5=3$.\nNote that 6 is the point of minima, not the minimum value.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following functions is strictly decreasing on $\\mathbb{R}$?","image":"","options":["$f(x) = e^{2x}$","$f(x) = 3x + 17$","$f(x) = 5 - 4x$","$f(x) = x^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$e^{2x}$: $f'(x) = 2e^{2x} > 0$, so it is increasing.\n$3x + 17$: $f'(x) = 3 > 0$, so it is increasing.\n$5 - 4x$: $f'(x) = -4 < 0$ for every x, so it is strictly decreasing on R.\n$x^2$: it decreases for $x < 0$ and increases for $x > 0$, so it is neither on R.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Minimum value of $|x + 3| - 2$\n(b) Maximum value of $\\sin 3x + 4$\n(c) Minimum value of $|\\sin 2x + 3|$\n(d) Maximum value of $\\frac{1}{x^2 + 4}$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{1}{4}$\n(ii) 2\n(iii) $-2$\n(iv) 5","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Since $|x + 3| \\ge 0$, the minimum is $0 - 2 = -2$ (at $x = -3$). So (a)-(iii).\n(b) The greatest value of $\\sin 3x$ is 1, so the maximum is $1 + 4 = 5$. So (b)-(iv).\n(c) $\\sin 2x + 3$ lies in $[2, 4]$, so it stays positive. Its least modulus is $|-1 + 3| = 2$. So (c)-(ii).\n(d) $x^2 + 4$ is least when $x = 0$, giving the maximum $\\frac{1}{4}$. So (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 100$ is increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) = 3(x-1)^2$, which is non-negative for all real x and vanishes only at the single point $x = 1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 6x + 3 = 3(x-1)^2 \\ge 0$. It is zero only at $x = 1$, so R is true.\nOn $(-\\infty, 1]$ and $[1, \\infty)$, $f' > 0$ except at an end point. So f is increasing on each part, hence on R. A is true.\nThe sign of $f'$ given in R is exactly what proves A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + 3x + 5$ is increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$. What is the greatest possible value of a?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 + 2ax + 3$. We need $f'(x) \\ge 0$ for all x, with equality at no more than isolated points.\nThe quadratic has a positive leading coefficient, so we need discriminant $\\le 0$: $4a^2 - 36 \\le 0$, giving $-3 \\le a \\le 3$.\nAt $a = 3$: $f'(x) = 3(x+1)^2 \\ge 0$, zero only at $x = -1$, so f is still increasing.\nGreatest a = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along the curve $y = x^2 - 4x$. At which point on the curve is the y-coordinate changing at the same rate as the x-coordinate, but in the opposite direction, i.e. $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -\\frac{dx}{dt}$ with $\\frac{dx}{dt} \\neq 0$?","image":"","options":["$\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{15}{4}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{7}{4}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate with respect to t: $\\frac{dy}{dt} = (2x - 4)\\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nSet $(2x - 4)\\frac{dx}{dt} = -\\frac{dx}{dt}$. Since $\\frac{dx}{dt} \\neq 0$, this gives $2x - 4 = -1$, so $x = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nThen $y = \\frac{9}{4} - 6 = -\\frac{15}{4}$.\nThe point $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$ satisfies $2x - 4 = +1$, where both coordinates change at the same rate in the same direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^3 + 3x$ on $[-1, 2]$. If $M$ and $m$ are the maximum and minimum values of $f$ on this interval, then $M - m$ equals","image":"","options":["10","14","18","22"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=x^3+3x$ on $[-1,2]$.\nFormula: $f'(x)=3x^2+3>0$ for all $x$, so $f$ is monotonic (increasing). A monotonic function takes its extrema at the end points.\nSubstituting: $M=f(2)=8+6=14$ and $m=f(-1)=-1-3=-4$.\nSo $M-m=14-(-4)=18$. Option 14 is wrong because it is $M$ alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = x^{2/3}$ on $[-1, 8]$.\nAssertion (A): The absolute minimum value of $f$ on $[-1, 8]$ is $0$, attained at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) = 0$ at $x = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = \\frac{2}{3}x^{-1/3}$, which is not defined at $x = 0$. So $x = 0$ is a critical point because $f$ is not differentiable there, not because $f' = 0$.\nIn fact $f'(x) \\neq 0$ for every $x$, so the only critical point is $x = 0$.\nValues: $f(-1) = 1$, $f(0) = 0$, $f(8) = 4$. The least is $0$ at $x = 0$, so A is true.\nR is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = |x|$ has a local minimum at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): At every point of local minimum of a function, its derivative is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: $|x| \\ge 0 = f(0)$ for all x, so 0 is a point of local (in fact absolute) minimum.\nR is false: $|x|$ is not differentiable at 0. Its left derivative is $-1$ and its right derivative is $+1$.\nNCERT's Theorem 2 says that at an extremum, either $f'(c) = 0$ or f is not differentiable at c.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A function $f$, differentiable on R, has derivative $f'(x) = (x-1)^2 (x+2)^3 (x-4)$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$x = -2$ is a point of local maxima, $x = 4$ is a point of local minima, and $x = 1$ is neither","$x = -2$ is a point of local minima, $x = 4$ is a point of local maxima, and $x = 1$ is a point of local minima","$x = 1$ and $x = 4$ are points of local minima and $x = -2$ is a point of local maxima","$x = -2$ and $x = 4$ are both points of local minima"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The critical points are $x = -2, 1, 4$.\nFor $x < -2$: $(x+2)^3 < 0$ and $(x-4) < 0$, so $f' > 0$.\nFor $-2 < x < 4$ (with $x \\neq 1$): $(x+2)^3 > 0$ and $(x-4) < 0$, so $f' < 0$.\nFor $x > 4$: $f' > 0$.\nAt $-2$ the sign of $f'$ changes from + to −, giving a local maximum. At $4$ it changes from − to +, giving a local minimum.\nThe factor $(x-1)^2$ never changes sign, so $f'$ does not change sign at $x = 1$. It is neither, i.e. a point of inflexion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x + \\frac{1}{x}$, $x \\neq 0$.\nAssertion (A): f is decreasing on the set $(-1, 0) \\cup (0, 1)$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) < 0$ for every x in $(-1, 0) \\cup (0, 1)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 1 - \\frac{1}{x^2} = \\frac{x^2-1}{x^2}$.\nFor $0 < |x| < 1$, we have $x^2 - 1 < 0$, so $f'(x) < 0$. Hence R is true.\nA: f is decreasing on $(-1,0)$ and on $(0,1)$ separately, but not on their union, because the union is not an interval.\nCheck: $f(-0.5) = -2.5$ and $f(0.5) = 2.5$. Here $-0.5 < 0.5$ but the value increases. So A is false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A right circular cylinder is inscribed in a sphere of radius $3\\sqrt{3}$ cm. The maximum possible volume of the cylinder is","image":"","options":["$108\\pi$ $cm^3$","$81\\pi$ $cm^3$","$72\\pi$ $cm^3$","$54\\pi$ $cm^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R=3\\sqrt3$, so $R^2=27$. If the cylinder has height $h$, its radius satisfies $r^2=R^2-\\frac{h^2}{4}$.\nFormula: $V=\\pi h\\left(27-\\frac{h^2}{4}\\right)$.\nDifferentiating: $V'=\\pi\\left(27-\\frac{3h^2}{4}\\right)=0$ gives $h=6$, which equals $\\frac{2R}{\\sqrt3}$.\n$V''=-\\frac{3\\pi h}{2}<0$, so this is a maximum.\nSubstituting: $r^2=27-9=18$, so $V=\\pi(18)(6)=108\\pi$ $cm^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $g(x) = \\log(1 + x) - \\frac{2x}{2 + x}$ for $x > -1$. Using the monotonicity of g, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\log(1 + x) > \\frac{2x}{2 + x}$ for all $x > 0$","$\\log(1 + x) < \\frac{2x}{2 + x}$ for all $x > 0$","$\\log(1 + x) > \\frac{2x}{2 + x}$ for all $x \\in (-1, 0)$","$g$ has a point of local minima at $x = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The derivative of $\\frac{2x}{2+x}$ is $\\frac{2(2+x) - 2x}{(2+x)^2} = \\frac{4}{(2+x)^2}$.\nStep 2: So $g'(x) = \\frac{1}{1+x} - \\frac{4}{(2+x)^2} = \\frac{(2+x)^2 - 4(1+x)}{(1+x)(2+x)^2} = \\frac{x^2}{(1+x)(2+x)^2}$.\nStep 3: For $x > -1$, $g'(x) \\geq 0$, with equality only at $x = 0$. So g is increasing on its domain, and $g(0) = 0$.\nStep 4: For $x > 0$, $g(x) > 0$, so (1) holds and (2) fails. For $-1 < x < 0$, $g(x) < 0$, so (3) fails.\nStep 5: $g'$ does not change sign at 0, so $x = 0$ is a point of inflexion, not a local minimum. So (4) fails.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = (x+2)^3 (x-4)^3$, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) f is decreasing on $(-\\infty, 1)$.\n(ii) f is increasing on $(1, \\infty)$.\n(iii) $x = -2$ is a point of local extremum of f.\n(iv) f has exactly three critical points.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate with the product rule: $f' = 3(x+2)^2(x-4)^3 + 3(x+2)^3(x-4)^2$.\nFactor: $f' = 3(x+2)^2(x-4)^2(2x-2) = 6(x+2)^2(x-4)^2(x-1)$. The squared factors are never negative, so the sign of $f'$ is the sign of $(x-1)$.\n(i) Correct: $f' \\leq 0$ for $x < 1$, zero only at the isolated point $-2$.\n(ii) Correct: $f' \\geq 0$ for $x > 1$, zero only at 4.\n(iii) Incorrect: $f'$ does not change sign at $-2$, so it is not an extremum.\n(iv) Correct: the critical points are $-2, 1, 4$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $f(x) = 3 + |x|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, has a local minimum value 3 at $x = 0$.\nStatement II: The second derivative test cannot be used to examine $f(x) = 3 + |x|$ at $x = 0$, because $f$ is not differentiable there.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"I: To the left of 0, $f(x) = 3 - x$, so $f' = -1 < 0$. To the right, $f(x) = 3 + x$, so $f' = 1 > 0$.\nBy the first derivative test, $x = 0$ is a local minimum with $f(0) = 3$. True.\nII: The second derivative test needs $f$ to be twice differentiable at $c$. $|x|$ is not even differentiable at 0, so the test cannot be applied. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The edge $x$ of a cube is increasing at the constant rate of 2 cm/s. At the instant when $x = 5$ cm, match each rate in List-I with its value in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Rate of increase of volume (in cm$^3$/s)\n(Q) Rate of increase of total surface area (in cm$^2$/s)\n(R) Rate of increase of the total length of all 12 edges (in cm/s)\n(S) Rate of increase of the diagonal of a face (in cm/s)\nList-II\n(1) 24\n(2) 120\n(3) 150\n(4) $2\\sqrt{2}$","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2$ cm/s and $x = 5$ cm.\n(P) $V = x^3$, so $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 3x^2\\frac{dx}{dt} = 3(25)(2) = 150$. P-3\n(Q) $S = 6x^2$, so $\\frac{dS}{dt} = 12x\\frac{dx}{dt} = 12(5)(2) = 120$. Q-2\n(R) $L = 12x$, so $\\frac{dL}{dt} = 12(2) = 24$. R-1\n(S) $d = \\sqrt{2}x$, so $\\frac{dd}{dt} = 2\\sqrt{2}$. S-4\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = 2x^3 - 6x^2 + 6x + 5$.\nStatement I: $x = 1$ is neither a point of local maxima nor a point of local minima of $f$; it is a point of inflexion.\nStatement II: Whenever $f'(c) = 0$ and $f''(c) = 0$, the point $x = c$ must be a point of inflexion.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Derivatives: $f'(x)=6(x-1)^2$ and $f''(x)=12(x-1)$. So $f'(1)=0$ and $f''(1)=0$, which means the second derivative test fails.\nApplying the first derivative test: $f'(x)>0$ on both sides of 1, so there is no sign change. Hence $x=1$ is a point of inflexion, and Statement I is true.\nStatement II: when $f''(c)=0$ the test only fails, and we must go back to the first derivative test. For example, $g(x)=x^4$ has $g'(0)=g''(0)=0$, yet $x=0$ is a local minimum. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A wire of length 44 cm is cut into two pieces. One piece is bent into a square and the other into a circle. The combined area enclosed is to be minimum. What is the length of the piece used for the circle? (Take $\\pi=\\frac{22}{7}$)","image":"","options":["19.36 cm","24.64 cm","22 cm","14 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the square piece have length $x$, so the circle piece is $44-x$.\nFormula: $A=\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)^2+\\frac{(44-x)^2}{4\\pi}$.\nDifferentiating: $A'=\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{44-x}{2\\pi}=0$, which gives $\\pi x=4(44-x)$, so $x=\\frac{176}{\\pi+4}$.\n$A''=\\frac18+\\frac{1}{2\\pi}>0$, so this is a minimum.\nSubstituting $\\pi=\\frac{22}{7}$: $x=\\frac{176\\times7}{50}=24.64$ cm.\nSo the circle piece is $44-24.64=19.36$ cm. (24.64 cm is the square piece.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = |x|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nStatement I: $f$ has a local minimum at $x = 0$ with local minimum value $0$.\nStatement II: $f$ is differentiable at $x = 0$ and $f'(0) = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for every $x$ and $f(0) = 0$, so $x = 0$ gives the least value, which is $0$.\nStatement II is false. The left-hand derivative at 0 is $-1$ and the right-hand derivative is $+1$, so $f$ is not differentiable at 0.\nThe point $x = 0$ is still a critical point, because a critical point includes any point where $f$ is not differentiable.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Water is poured at $6\\pi$ m$^3$/h into an inverted right circular conical tank. The tank's axis is vertical, its vertex is at the bottom, and its semi-vertical angle is $\\tan^{-1}(0.5)$. When the water is 6 m deep, at what rate is the area of the circular water surface increasing?","image":"","options":["$2\\pi$ m$^2$/h","$\\pi$ m$^2$/h","$4\\pi$ m$^2$/h","$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ m$^2$/h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since $\\frac{r}{h} = 0.5$, the water surface radius is $r = \\frac{h}{2}$, so $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{\\pi h^3}{12}$.\nThen $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi h^2}{4}\\frac{dh}{dt}$, which gives $6\\pi = \\frac{\\pi(36)}{4}\\frac{dh}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dh}{dt} = \\frac{2}{3}$ m/h.\nSurface area: $A = \\pi r^2 = \\frac{\\pi h^2}{4}$, so $\\frac{dA}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi h}{2}\\frac{dh}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi(6)}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3} = 2\\pi$ m$^2$/h.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x + 2\\cos x$, $x \\in [0, \\pi]$.\nStatement I: The absolute maximum value of $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\sqrt{3}$.\nStatement II: The absolute minimum value of $f$ is $\\pi - 2$, attained at the end point $x = \\pi$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 1 - 2\\sin x = 0$ gives $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}$, so $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ or $x = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$.\nValues: $f(0) = 2$, $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\sqrt{3} \\approx 2.26$, $f\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{5\\pi}{6} - \\sqrt{3} \\approx 0.89$, $f(\\pi) = \\pi - 2 \\approx 1.14$.\nThe maximum is $\\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\sqrt{3}$, so I is true.\nThe minimum is $\\frac{5\\pi}{6} - \\sqrt{3}$, at an interior point, so II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"At how many points in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ does the function $f(x) = \\cos 2x$ attain its maximum value?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The maximum value of $\\cos 2x$ is $1$, and it occurs when $2x = 2n\\pi$.\nFor $x \\in [0, 2\\pi]$, we have $2x \\in [0, 4\\pi]$, so $2x = 0, 2\\pi, 4\\pi$.\nThis gives $x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi$. Both end points are included because the interval is closed.\nSo the maximum is attained at 3 points. Forgetting the end points leads to the wrong count of 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"At how many points of the interval $[0, 3\\pi]$ does the function $f(x) = \\cos 2x$ attain its absolute maximum value?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The greatest value of $\\cos 2x$ is 1. It occurs when $2x = 2n\\pi$, i.e. $x = n\\pi$.\nFor $x \\in [0, 3\\pi]$ we get $x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, 3\\pi$.\nThe end points 0 and $3\\pi$ count, because the working rule always checks end points.\nSo the maximum value 1 is attained at 4 points.\nOption 2 is tempting only if you drop both end points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"An isosceles triangle has a fixed base of 8 cm. Each of its two equal sides is decreasing at 2 cm/s. At what rate is the area decreasing at the instant each equal side is 5 cm?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{20}{3}$ cm$^2$/s","$\\frac{40}{3}$ cm$^2$/s","10 cm$^2$/s","$\\frac{80}{3}$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: base 8 cm, equal side $a$, $\\frac{da}{dt} = -2$ cm/s and $a = 5$ cm.\nThe height is $\\sqrt{a^2 - 16}$, so area $A = \\frac{1}{2}(8)\\sqrt{a^2-16} = 4\\sqrt{a^2-16}$.\nDifferentiating: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = \\frac{4a}{\\sqrt{a^2-16}}\\frac{da}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = \\frac{4(5)}{3}(-2) = -\\frac{40}{3}$.\nSo the area decreases at $\\frac{40}{3}$ cm$^2$/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Gas is pumped into a spherical balloon at 400 cm$^3$/s, and the balloon stays spherical. At the instant its radius is 10 cm, at what rate is its surface area increasing?","image":"","options":["40 cm$^2$/s","160 cm$^2$/s","80 cm$^2$/s","$80\\pi$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 400$ cm$^3$/s and $r = 10$ cm.\nFind the radius rate: $V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, so $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 4\\pi r^2\\frac{dr}{dt}$. This gives $\\frac{dr}{dt} = \\frac{400}{400\\pi} = \\frac{1}{\\pi}$ cm/s.\nFind the area rate: $S = 4\\pi r^2$, so $\\frac{dS}{dt} = 8\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt} = 8\\pi(10)\\left(\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\right) = 80$ cm$^2$/s.\nThe $\\pi$ cancels, so $80\\pi$ is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Find the rate of change of the area of a circle with respect to its circumference at the instant when the area is $49\\pi$ cm$^2$.","image":"","options":["7 cm","14 cm","3.5 cm","$14\\pi$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\pi r^2 = 49\\pi$, so r = 7 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{dA}{dC} = \\frac{dA/dr}{dC/dr} = \\frac{2\\pi r}{2\\pi} = r$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dC} = 7$ cm.\nThe option $14\\pi$ cm is $\\frac{dA}{dr}$, which is the rate with respect to the radius, not the circumference.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A right circular cylinder is inscribed in a right circular cone of height 12 cm and base radius 6 cm. The axes of the cylinder and the cone coincide, and the base of the cylinder lies on the base of the cone. What is the maximum possible volume of the cylinder?","image":"","options":["$64\\pi$ cm$^3$","$96\\pi$ cm$^3$","$48\\pi$ cm$^3$","$128\\pi$ cm$^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the cylinder have radius $x$. By similar triangles, its height is $h = \\frac{12(6 - x)}{6} = 12 - 2x$.\nVolume: $V = \\pi x^2(12 - 2x) = \\pi(12x^2 - 2x^3)$.\nDifferentiate: $V' = \\pi(24x - 6x^2) = 0$ gives $x = 4$.\nCheck: $V'' = \\pi(24 - 12x)$, so $V''(4) = -24\\pi < 0$, confirming a maximum.\nAt $x = 4$, $h = 4$ (one-third of the cone's height), so $V = \\pi(16)(4) = 64\\pi$ cm$^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of all rectangles inscribed in a fixed circle, the square has the maximum area.\nStatement II: Of all closed right circular cylinders of a given volume, the one with minimum total surface area has its height equal to its radius.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Take the sides as $2r\\cos\\theta$ and $2r\\sin\\theta$, so the area is $A=2r^2\\sin2\\theta$. This is maximum at $\\theta=45^\\circ$, where the two sides are equal, giving a square.\nStatement II is false. With $V=\\pi r^2h$ fixed, the surface area is $S=2\\pi r^2+\\frac{2V}{r}$. Setting $S'=4\\pi r-\\frac{2V}{r^2}=0$ gives $V=2\\pi r^3$. Hence $h=2r$, so the height equals the diameter, not the radius.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function) with List-II (interval on which the function is strictly decreasing).\nList-I:\n(a) $x^2 - 6x + 1$\n(b) $4 - 5x - x^2$\n(c) $7 - 4x - 2x^2$\n(d) $x^2 + 8x$\nList-II:\n(p) $(-1, \\infty)$\n(q) $(-\\infty, -4)$\n(r) $(-\\infty, 3)$\n(s) $(-\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty)$","image":"","options":["(a)-r, (b)-s, (c)-p, (d)-q","(a)-r, (b)-p, (c)-s, (d)-q","(a)-q, (b)-s, (c)-p, (d)-r","(a)-s, (b)-r, (c)-q, (d)-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $f' = 2x - 6 < 0$ when $x < 3$, which matches (r).\n(b) $f' = -5 - 2x < 0$ when $x > -\\frac{5}{2}$, which matches (s).\n(c) $f' = -4 - 4x < 0$ when $x > -1$, which matches (p).\n(d) $f' = 2x + 8 < 0$ when $x < -4$, which matches (q).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity, r = radius) with List-II (its rate of change with respect to r).\nList-I:\n(A) Area of a circle\n(B) Circumference of a circle\n(C) Volume of a sphere\n(D) Surface area of a sphere\nList-II:\n(1) $4\\pi r^2$\n(2) $2\\pi$\n(3) $8\\pi r$\n(4) $2\\pi r$","image":"","options":["A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{d}{dr}(\\pi r^2) = 2\\pi r$, so A matches (4).\n(B) $\\frac{d}{dr}(2\\pi r) = 2\\pi$, so B matches (2).\n(C) $\\frac{d}{dr}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) = 4\\pi r^2$, so C matches (1).\n(D) $\\frac{d}{dr}(4\\pi r^2) = 8\\pi r$, so D matches (3).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Gas is pumped into a spherical balloon at the rate of $400\\pi$ cm$^3$/s, and the balloon stays spherical. At what rate is its surface area increasing when its radius is 10 cm?","image":"","options":["$40\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$160\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$20\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$80\\pi$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 400\\pi$ cm$^3$/s and $r = 10$ cm.\nVolume: $V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, so $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 4\\pi r^2\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nThis gives $400\\pi = 4\\pi(100)\\frac{dr}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 1$ cm/s.\nSurface area: $S = 4\\pi r^2$, so $\\frac{dS}{dt} = 8\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt} = 8\\pi(10)(1) = 80\\pi$ cm$^2$/s.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A man 1.6 m tall walks away from the foot of a 4.8 m high lamp post at a uniform speed of 6 km/h. At what rate is the length of his shadow increasing?","image":"","options":["2 km/h","9 km/h","3 km/h","4.5 km/h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: man's height 1.6 m, lamp height 4.8 m, $\\frac{dl}{dt} = 6$ km/h, where $l$ is the man's distance from the post and $s$ is his shadow length.\nFormula: by similar triangles, $\\frac{s}{l+s} = \\frac{1.6}{4.8} = \\frac{1}{3}$, so $l + s = 3s$, giving $l = 2s$.\nDifferentiating: $\\frac{dl}{dt} = 2\\frac{ds}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{ds}{dt} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$ km/h.\n(9 km/h is the speed of the shadow's tip, not the rate of change of its length.)\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The edge $x$ of a cube is increasing at the rate of $3$ cm/s. At the instant when $x = 10$ cm, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The volume is increasing at $900$ cm$^3$/s.\n(ii) The total surface area is increasing at $360$ cm$^2$/s.\n(iii) The sum of the lengths of all edges is increasing at $12$ cm/s.\n(iv) The rate of increase of volume with respect to time is independent of $x$.","image":"","options":["1","3","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 3x^2 \\frac{dx}{dt} = 3(100)(3) = 900$ cm$^3$/s.\n(ii) Correct: $\\frac{dS}{dt} = 12x \\frac{dx}{dt} = 12(10)(3) = 360$ cm$^2$/s.\n(iii) Incorrect: total edge length is $L = 12x$, so $\\frac{dL}{dt} = 12 \\times 3 = 36$ cm/s, not 12 cm/s.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 9x^2$ depends on $x$.\nSo two statements, (i) and (ii), are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions have neither a local maximum nor a local minimum anywhere on their domain?\n(i) $e^{2x}$\n(ii) $\\log x,\\ x > 0$\n(iii) $x^3 + x^2 + x + 1$\n(iv) $x^3 - 3x$\n(v) $|x - 1|$\n(vi) $x^5$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Derivative $2e^{2x} > 0$ always, so no extrema.\n(ii) Derivative $\\frac{1}{x} > 0$, so no extrema.\n(iii) Derivative $3x^2 + 2x + 1$ has discriminant $4 - 12 < 0$, so it is always positive and there are no extrema.\n(iv) Derivative $3(x-1)(x+1)$ changes sign, so there is a local max at $-1$ and a local min at $1$.\n(v) Local min at $x = 1$, where the function is not differentiable.\n(vi) Derivative $5x^4 \\ge 0$ never changes sign, so $x = 0$ is a point of inflexion.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A curve is given parametrically by $x = t^2 + 1$ and $y = t^3 - 3t$.\nStatement I: The rate of change of $y$ with respect to $x$ at $t = 2$ is $\\frac{9}{4}$.\nStatement II: The formula $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$ can be used to find the rate of change of $y$ with respect to $x$ at $t = 0$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate both with respect to $t$: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2 - 3$.\nStatement I: at $t = 2$, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 4$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 12 - 3 = 9$. So $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{9}{4}$. True.\nStatement II: the chain rule form needs $\\frac{dx}{dt} \\neq 0$. At $t = 0$, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 0$, so the formula cannot be applied. False.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The radius of a circle increases at a constant rate of 2 cm/s. At what rate is its circumference increasing?","image":"","options":["$\\pi$ cm/s","$2\\pi$ cm/s","$4\\pi$ cm/s","$8\\pi$ cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 2$ cm/s.\nFormula: $C = 2\\pi r$, so $\\frac{dC}{dt} = 2\\pi \\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dC}{dt} = 2\\pi(2) = 4\\pi$ cm/s.\nThe rate does not depend on r, because C is linear in r.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The shortest distance of the point $\\left(0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ from the parabola $y=x^2$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{2}$","$\\frac{5}{2}$","$\\sqrt{2}$","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take a point $(h,k)$ on the parabola, so $k=h^2$.\nThen $D^2=h^2+(k-c)^2=k+\\left(k-\\frac52\\right)^2$, with $c=\\frac{5}{2}$.\nDifferentiating with respect to $k$: $1+2\\left(k-\\frac52\\right)=0$ gives $k=2$.\nThe derivative changes from − to +, so this is a minimum.\nSubstituting: $D^2=2+\\frac14=\\frac94$, so $D=\\frac32$.\nThis agrees with $\\frac{\\sqrt{4c-1}}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$. The value $\\frac52$ is the distance to the vertex, which is not the nearest point.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x$. For any real numbers a and b with $a < b$, which of the following must always hold?","image":"","options":["$f(b) - f(a) \\ge b - a$","$f(b) - f(a) \\le b - a$","$f(b) - f(a) \\ge 4(b - a)$","$f(b) = f(a)$ for some choice of $a < b$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"We have $f'(x) = 3(x-1)^2 + 1 \\ge 1$ for all x.\nBy MVT, $f(b) - f(a) = f'(c)(b - a)$ for some $c \\in (a, b)$. Since $f'(c) \\ge 1$ and $b - a > 0$, we get $f(b) - f(a) \\ge b - a$.\nOption (3) fails. Near $x = 1$, $f' \\approx 1$: $f(1.1) - f(0.9) = 0.202 < 0.8$.\nOption (4) fails because f is strictly increasing. Option (2) contradicts the result.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x^2 (x-3)^3$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, which of the following correctly describes its critical points?","image":"","options":["Local minimum at $x = 0$, local maximum at $x = \\frac{6}{5}$, point of inflexion at $x = 3$","Local maximum at $x = 0$, local minimum at $x = 3$, point of inflexion at $x = \\frac{6}{5}$","Local maximum at $x = 0$, local minimum at $x = \\frac{6}{5}$, point of inflexion at $x = 3$","Local minimum at $x = \\frac{6}{5}$, local maximum at $x = 3$, point of inflexion at $x = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 2x(x-3)^3 + 3x^2(x-3)^2 = x(x-3)^2(5x - 6)$.\nThe critical points are $x = 0$, $\\frac{6}{5}$ and $3$.\nFor $x < 0$: the signs are $(-)(+)(-)$, so $f' > 0$. For $0 < x < \\frac{6}{5}$: $(+)(+)(-)$, so $f' < 0$. Hence $x = 0$ is a local maximum.\nFor $\\frac{6}{5} < x < 3$: $f' > 0$. Hence $x = \\frac{6}{5}$ is a local minimum.\nFor $x > 3$: $f' > 0$ still. Since $(x-3)^2$ does not change sign, $x = 3$ is a point of inflexion.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A differentiable function $f$ has derivative $f'(x) = (x-1)^2 (x-2)(x+3)$. Which of the following is correct according to the first derivative test?","image":"","options":["$x=-3$ is a point of local maxima, $x=2$ is a point of local minima, and $x=1$ is neither","$x=-3$ is a point of local minima, $x=2$ is a point of local maxima, and $x=1$ is a point of local minima","$x=-3$ and $x=1$ are points of local maxima, and $x=2$ is a point of local minima","$x=-3$ is a point of local maxima and $x=1$ and $x=2$ are both points of local minima"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The factor $(x-1)^2\\ge0$ never changes sign, so only $(x-2)(x+3)$ decides the sign.\nFor $x<-3$: $f'>0$. For $-3<x<2$ with $x\\neq1$: $f'<0$. For $x>2$: $f'>0$.\nAt $x=-3$ the sign goes $+\\to-$, so it is a local maxima.\nAt $x=2$ the sign goes $-\\to+$, so it is a local minima.\nAt $x=1$, $f'$ stays negative on both sides, so it is neither (a point of inflexion).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions have neither a local maximum nor a local minimum on their natural domains?\n$e^x$,  $\\log x$,  $x^3+x^2+x+1$,  $x^3-3x$,  $|x|$,  $\\frac{1}{x^2+2}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$e^x$: $f'=e^x>0$ everywhere, so there is no extremum.\n$\\log x$: $f'=\\frac{1}{x}>0$ for $x>0$, so there is no extremum.\n$x^3+x^2+x+1$: $f'=3x^2+2x+1$ has discriminant $4-12<0$, so $f'>0$ always and there is no extremum.\n$x^3-3x$: $f'=3(x-1)(x+1)$ changes sign, so it has a local maximum at $-1$ and a local minimum at $1$.\n$|x|$: it has a local minimum at $0$.\n$\\frac{1}{x^2+2}$: it has a maximum $\\frac{1}{2}$ at $x=0$.\nSo exactly 3 functions have no extrema.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A man 1.5 m tall walks away from the foot of a 6 m high lamp post at a uniform speed of 4 km/h on level ground.\nStatement I: The length of his shadow increases at the rate of $\\frac{4}{3}$ km/h.\nStatement II: The tip of his shadow moves away from the lamp post at 4 km/h.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let the man be $l$ from the post and let his shadow have length $s$. By similar triangles, $\\frac{s}{l + s} = \\frac{1.5}{6}$, so $4s = l + s$, which gives $s = \\frac{l}{3}$.\nStatement I: $\\frac{ds}{dt} = \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{dl}{dt} = \\frac{4}{3}$ km/h. Correct.\nStatement II: The tip is at distance $l + s$ from the post, so it moves at $\\frac{dl}{dt} + \\frac{ds}{dt} = 4 + \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{16}{3}$ km/h, not 4 km/h. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = \\sin^2 x + \\cos x$, $x \\in [0, \\pi]$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Absolute maximum value of $f$\n(Q) Absolute minimum value of $f$\n(R) Point where the absolute maximum is attained\n(S) Point where the absolute minimum is attained\nList-II\n(1) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$\n(2) $-1$\n(3) $\\frac{5}{4}$\n(4) $\\pi$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1","P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 2\\sin x\\cos x - \\sin x = \\sin x(2\\cos x - 1)$.\nSo $f'(x) = 0$ at $x = 0$, $\\pi$ (where $\\sin x = 0$) and at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ (where $\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}$).\nValues: $f(0) = 1$, $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{5}{4}$, $f(\\pi) = 0 - 1 = -1$.\nSo the maximum is $\\frac{5}{4}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ (P-3, R-1), and the minimum is $-1$ at $x = \\pi$ (Q-2, S-4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = 2x^3 - 9x^2 + 12x + 1$ is strictly decreasing on","image":"","options":["$(-\\infty, 1)$","$(2, \\infty)$","$(1, 2)$","$(-2, -1)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 6x^2 - 18x + 12 = 6(x-1)(x-2)$.\nThe critical points $x = 1$ and $x = 2$ divide the line into $(-\\infty, 1)$, $(1, 2)$ and $(2, \\infty)$.\nSign of $f'$ on each piece: positive, negative, positive.\nSo f decreases only on $(1, 2)$. On $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2, \\infty)$ it increases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Height of the cone of maximum volume inscribed in a sphere of radius $R$\n(B) Height of the cylinder of maximum volume inscribed in a sphere of radius $R$\n(C) Radius of the cylinder of greatest curved surface area inscribed in a cone of base radius $r$\n(D) Height of the cylinder of greatest volume inscribed in a cone of height $h$\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{r}{2}$\n(II) $\\frac{4R}{3}$\n(III) $\\frac{h}{3}$\n(IV) $\\frac{2R}{\\sqrt3}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Volume $V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}(2Rh-h^2)h$. $V'=0$ gives $4Rh=3h^2$, so $h=\\frac{4R}{3}$: (II).\n(B) Volume $V=\\pi\\left(R^2h-\\frac{h^3}{4}\\right)$. $V'=0$ gives $h=\\frac{2R}{\\sqrt3}$: (IV).\n(C) Curved surface $S\\propto rx-x^2$, maximum at $x=\\frac{r}{2}$: (I).\n(D) Volume $V=\\pi x^2h\\left(1-\\frac{x}{r}\\right)$, maximum at $x=\\frac{2r}{3}$, so the height is $\\frac{h}{3}$: (III).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $x$ be the edge of a cube whose edge increases at a constant rate.\nStatement I: The volume of the cube also increases at a constant rate.\nStatement II: $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 3x^2 \\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II: Since $V = x^3$, the chain rule gives $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 3x^2\\frac{dx}{dt}$. It is true.\nStatement I: Here $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ is constant, but $x$ keeps growing. So $\\frac{dV}{dt} = 3x^2\\frac{dx}{dt}$ keeps growing too. The volume rate is not constant, so Statement I is false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = x^3 - 6x^2 + 12x - 5$ is an increasing function on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nReason (R): $f'(x) > 0$ for every $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12x + 12 = 3(x-2)^2$.\nSo $f'(x) \\ge 0$ for all x, and $f'(x) = 0$ only at the single point $x = 2$.\nA is true: for $x_1 < x_2$, $f(x) = (x-2)^3 + 3$ gives $(x_1-2)^3 < (x_2-2)^3$, so $f(x_1) < f(x_2)$.\nR is false, because $f'(2) = 0$, which is not positive.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = \\sin 3x$ for $x \\in \\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$. On which of the following is f increasing?","image":"","options":["$\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right]$","$\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$","$\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right]$","$\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3\\cos 3x$.\nSetting $f'(x) = 0$ gives $\\cos 3x = 0$. Since $3x \\in \\left[0, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$, the root inside the interval is $3x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, that is, $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\nFor $0 \\le x < \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, we have $0 \\le 3x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $\\cos 3x > 0$ and f is increasing. f is continuous at the end points, so it is increasing on $\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right]$.\nFor $\\frac{\\pi}{6} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\cos 3x < 0$, so f is decreasing. This rules out $\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right]$ and the other options.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the derivative can be written as $f'(x) = 3(x-1)^2 + c$ by completing the square. What is the least value of $f'(x)$ over all real $x$?","image":"","options":["0","1","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 6x + 4$.\nComplete the square: $3(x^2 - 2x + 1) + 1 = 3(x-1)^2 + 1$.\nSince $3(x-1)^2 \\ge 0$, we get $f'(x) \\ge 1$. Equality holds at $x = 1$.\nSo the least value is 1. This is why f is increasing on R: $f'(x) > 0$ everywhere.\nThe value 4 is $f'(0)$, not the minimum.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The profit (in thousand rupees) of a firm producing $x$ units is $P(x)=50+24x-3x^2$. The maximum profit (in thousand rupees) is","image":"","options":["98","146","50","74"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P(x)=50+24x-3x^2$.\nDifferentiating: $P'(x)=24-6x=0$ gives $x=4$.\n$P''(x)=-6<0$, so $x=4$ gives the maximum.\nSubstituting: $P(4)=50+96-48=98$.\nSo the maximum profit is 98 thousand rupees. The value 146 wrongly ignores the $-3x^2$ term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $f'(c) = 0$, then $c$ must be a point of local maxima or local minima of $f$.\nStatement II: If $f$, defined on an open interval, has a local maxima or local minima at $x = c$, then either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f$ is not differentiable at $c$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Take $f(x)=x^3$: $f'(0)=0$, but 0 is not an extremum. So the converse of Theorem 2 does not hold.\nStatement II is true. It is exactly NCERT's Theorem 2. An example where $f$ is not differentiable is $|x|$, which has a local minimum at 0.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Two positive numbers have a sum of 30. What is the largest possible value of their product?","image":"","options":["225","200","216","450"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the numbers be $x$ and $30-x$. The product is $P(x)=30x-x^2$.\nThen $P'(x)=30-2x=0$, which gives $x=15$.\nAlso $P''(x)=-2<0$, so $x=15$ is a point of maxima.\nThe numbers are 15 and 15, and the maximum product is $15\\times15=225$.\nOption (2), 200, is the product of 10 and 20, which is not the best split.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = x e^{-2x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, is increasing on the interval","image":"","options":["$\\left(-\\infty, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\infty\\right)$","$(-\\infty, 2)$","$(2, \\infty)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the product rule, $f'(x) = e^{-2x} - 2x e^{-2x} = e^{-2x}(1 - 2x)$.\nSince $e^{-2x} > 0$ for all x, the sign of $f'(x)$ is the sign of $1 - 2x$.\n$f'(x) > 0$ when $x < \\frac{1}{2}$, so f is increasing on $\\left(-\\infty, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n$(-\\infty, 2)$ is tempting but wrong: it includes $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\right)$, where $f' < 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x^2 - x + 1$ on the interval $(-1, 1)$, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["f is strictly increasing on $(-1, 1)$ because $f(1) > f(-1)$","f is strictly decreasing on $(-1, 1)$ because $f(0) < f(-1)$","f is neither strictly increasing nor strictly decreasing on $(-1, 1)$ because $f'(x)$ changes sign at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$","f is constant on $(-1, 1)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 2x - 1$, which is zero at $x = \\frac{1}{2}$, a point inside $(-1, 1)$.\nOn $(-1, \\frac{1}{2})$, $f' < 0$, so f decreases. On $(\\frac{1}{2}, 1)$, $f' > 0$, so f increases.\nSince the derivative changes sign within the interval, f is neither strictly increasing nor strictly decreasing there.\nOption (1) fails because comparing only the two endpoints does not prove monotonicity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following functions is strictly increasing on the whole of $\\mathbb{R}$?","image":"","options":["$f(x) = 5 - 2x$","$f(x) = x^2$","$f(x) = e^{-x}$","$f(x) = x^3 + 2x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Use Theorem 1: if $f'(x) > 0$ on an interval, then f is increasing there.\n(1) $f'(x) = -2 < 0$, so f is decreasing on R.\n(2) $f'(x) = 2x$, which is negative for $x<0$ and positive for $x>0$. So f is neither increasing nor decreasing on R.\n(3) $f'(x) = -e^{-x} < 0$, so f is decreasing on R.\n(4) $f'(x) = 3x^2 + 2 \\ge 2 > 0$ for every real x, so f is strictly increasing on R.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = x^4 - 50x^2 + ax + 3$ attains its absolute maximum value on $[0, 2]$ at $x = 1$. The value of $a$ is","image":"","options":["96","104","-96","100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$x = 1$ is an interior point of $[0, 2]$, so by Theorem 6, $f'(1) = 0$.\n$f'(x) = 4x^3 - 100x + a$, so $f'(1) = 4 - 100 + a = 0$, giving $a = 96$.\nCheck: $f'(x) = 4(x-1)(x^2 + x - 24)$. Its other roots, $\\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{97}}{2}$, lie outside $[0, 2]$.\nValues: $f(0) = 3$, $f(1) = 50$, $f(2) = 11$. So the maximum really is at $x = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $g(x) = \\log x - \\frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}$ for $x > 0$. Using the sign of $g'(x)$, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\log x > \\frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}$ for every $x > 1$","$\\log x < \\frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}$ for every $x > 1$","g is decreasing on $(0, 1)$","g has a local maximum at $x = 1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}\\right] = \\frac{4}{(x+1)^2}$.\nSo $g'(x) = \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{4}{(x+1)^2} = \\frac{(x+1)^2 - 4x}{x(x+1)^2} = \\frac{(x-1)^2}{x(x+1)^2} \\ge 0$.\n$g'$ is zero only at $x = 1$, so g is increasing on $(0, \\infty)$. This rules out (3), and also (4), since $g'$ does not change sign.\nFor $x > 1$: $g(x) > g(1) = 0$, so $\\log x > \\frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = (x-1)^3 (x+2)^4$, which of the following correctly classifies all its critical points?","image":"","options":["Local maxima at $x=-2$, local minima at $x=-\\frac{2}{7}$, point of inflexion at $x=1$","Local minima at $x=-2$, local maxima at $x=-\\frac{2}{7}$, point of inflexion at $x=1$","Local maxima at $x=-2$, local minima at $x=1$, point of inflexion at $x=-\\frac{2}{7}$","Local maxima at $x=-\\frac{2}{7}$, local minima at $x=1$, point of inflexion at $x=-2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First derivative: $f'(x)=3(x-1)^2(x+2)^4+4(x-1)^3(x+2)^3=(x-1)^2(x+2)^3(7x+2)$.\nCritical points: $x=1,\\ -2,\\ -\\frac{2}{7}$.\nSign of $f'$: the factor $(x-1)^2\\ge 0$, so the sign depends on $(x+2)^3(7x+2)$. It is $+$ for $x<-2$, $-$ on $\\left(-2,-\\frac{2}{7}\\right)$, and $+$ for $x>-\\frac{2}{7}$.\nAt $x=-2$ the sign changes $+$ to $-$, giving a local maximum.\nAt $x=-\\frac{2}{7}$ the sign changes $-$ to $+$, giving a local minimum.\nAt $x=1$ there is no sign change, so it is a point of inflexion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function on the given interval) with List-II (derivative and nature).\nList-I:\n(P) $\\log(\\sin x)$ on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$\n(Q) $\\log|\\cos x|$ on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n(R) $\\tan^{-1}(\\sin x + \\cos x)$ on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n(S) $\\cos x$ on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$\nList-II:\n(1) $f'(x) = -\\sin x$, increasing\n(2) $f'(x) = \\cot x$, decreasing\n(3) $f'(x) = \\frac{\\cos x - \\sin x}{2 + \\sin 2x}$, increasing\n(4) $f'(x) = -\\tan x$, decreasing","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\log \\sin x = \\cot x$, which is negative on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$. Decreasing, so P-2.\n(Q) Here $\\cos x > 0$, so the derivative is $\\frac{-\\sin x}{\\cos x} = -\\tan x < 0$. Decreasing, so Q-4.\n(R) The derivative is $\\frac{\\cos x - \\sin x}{1 + (\\sin x + \\cos x)^2} = \\frac{\\cos x - \\sin x}{2 + \\sin 2x}$. It is positive because $\\cos x > \\sin x$ on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. Increasing, so R-3.\n(S) The derivative is $-\\sin x$, which is positive since $\\sin x < 0$ on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$. Increasing, so S-1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many integer values of k make the function $f(x) = x^3 + kx^2 + 3x + 1$ increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$?","image":"","options":["5","6","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 + 2kx + 3$.\nFor f to be increasing on R we need $f'(x) \\ge 0$ for all x. This quadratic opens upward, so it must have discriminant $\\le 0$: $4k^2 - 36 \\le 0$, which gives $-3 \\le k \\le 3$.\nCheck the end values: at $k = \\pm 3$, $f'(x) = 3(x \\pm 1)^2$. This is zero at only one point, so f is still strictly increasing.\nThe allowed integers are $-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3$. That is 7 values.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = 3x^{2/3} - 2x$, $x \\in [-1, 8]$. If $M$ and $m$ are the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of $f$ respectively, then $M - m$ equals","image":"","options":["9","5","1","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Find critical points: $f'(x) = 2x^{-1/3} - 2 = \\frac{2(1 - x^{1/3})}{x^{1/3}}$.\n$f'(x) = 0$ gives $x = 1$. $f'$ is undefined at $x = 0$, so 0 is also a critical point.\nEvaluate at critical points and end points:\n$f(-1) = 3(1) + 2 = 5$\n$f(0) = 0$\n$f(1) = 1$\n$f(8) = 3(4) - 16 = -4$\nSo $M = 5$ (at $x = -1$) and $m = -4$ (at $x = 8$), giving $M - m = 9$.\nOption 5 is tempting if you wrongly take $(-1)^{2/3} = -1$ or skip the end point $-1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = 3 + |x|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, has a local minimum value 3 at $x = 0$.\nReason (R): $f'(0) = 0$ and $f''(0) > 0$, so the second derivative test confirms a local minimum at $x=0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Checking A: to the left of 0, $f(x)=3-x$, so $f'(x)=-1<0$. To the right of 0, $f(x)=3+x$, so $f'(x)=1>0$. Thus $f'$ changes sign from negative to positive at 0, and the first derivative test gives a local minimum $f(0)=3$. A is true.\nChecking R: $f$ is not differentiable at $x=0$, so neither $f'(0)$ nor $f''(0)$ exists. The second derivative test cannot be applied here. R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The total revenue (in rupees) from selling x units of a product is $R(x) = 2x^2 + 10x + 3$. What is the marginal revenue when $x = 4$?","image":"","options":["₹ 26","₹ 18","₹ 75","₹ 42"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Marginal revenue is the rate of change of total revenue with respect to the number of units sold.\nFormula: $MR = \\frac{dR}{dx} = 4x + 10$.\nSubstituting $x = 4$: $MR = 16 + 10 = 26$.\n₹ 75 is the total revenue $R(4)$, not the marginal revenue.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"In the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$, the function $f(x) = \\sin x - \\cos x$ is decreasing on","image":"","options":["$\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right]$","$\\left[\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}\\right]$","$\\left[0, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right]$","$\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = \\cos x + \\sin x = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.\nSetting $f'(x) = 0$ in $[0, 2\\pi]$ gives $x = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $x = \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$.\nFor $\\frac{3\\pi}{4} < x < \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$, we have $\\pi < x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} < 2\\pi$, so the sine is negative and $f'(x) < 0$.\nf is continuous, so it is decreasing on $\\left[\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}\\right]$.\nThe tempting option $\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right]$ is the decreasing interval of $\\sin x + \\cos x$, not of this function.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Positive numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x+y=21$, and the product $x^2y^5$ is as large as possible. The value of $y-x$ is","image":"","options":["9","3","5","15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $f(x)=x^2(21-x)^5$.\nDifferentiating: $f'(x)=2x(21-x)^5-5x^2(21-x)^4=x(21-x)^4[2(21-x)-5x]$.\nFor $0<x<21$, $f'(x)=0$ gives $42-7x=0$, so $x=6$.\n$f'$ changes from + to − at $x=6$, so it is a maximum.\nThen $y=15$, and $y-x=9$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The radius of a circular metal ring increases at 0.4 cm/s on heating. At the instant when its circumference is $30\\pi$ cm, at what rate is the area enclosed by the ring increasing?","image":"","options":["$12\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$6\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$24\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$0.8\\pi$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 0.4$ cm/s and $2\\pi r = 30\\pi$, so r = 15 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi(15)(0.4) = 12\\pi$.\nSo the area increases at $12\\pi$ cm$^2$/s.\nThe option $6\\pi$ cm$^2$/s comes from forgetting the factor 2 in $2\\pi r$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Each of two positive numbers whose sum is $20$ and the sum of whose squares is minimum\n(b) Maximum product of two numbers whose sum is $24$\n(c) Maximum value of $\\sin x + \\cos x$\n(d) Minimum value of $9x^2 + 12x + 2$\nList-II\n(p) $\\sqrt{2}$\n(q) $144$\n(r) $10$\n(s) $-2$","image":"","options":["(a)-(r), (b)-(q), (c)-(p), (d)-(s)","(a)-(q), (b)-(r), (c)-(p), (d)-(s)","(a)-(r), (b)-(q), (c)-(s), (d)-(p)","(a)-(p), (b)-(q), (c)-(r), (d)-(s)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Let $S = x^2 + (20-x)^2$. Then $S' = 4x - 40 = 0$ gives $x = 10$, and $S'' = 4 > 0$. Each number is $10$, so (a)-(r).\n(b) Let $P = x(24-x)$. Then $P' = 24 - 2x = 0$ gives $x = 12$, and $P = 144$. So (b)-(q).\n(c) With $f' = \\cos x - \\sin x = 0$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, the value is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2} = \\sqrt2$. So (c)-(p).\n(d) With $f' = 18x + 12 = 0$, $x = -\\frac{2}{3}$, and $f = 4 - 8 + 2 = -2$. So (d)-(s).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The local maximum value of $f(x) = x\\sqrt{4 - x}$, $0 < x < 4$, is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{9}$","$\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}$","$\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{9}$","$\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{27}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First derivative: $f'(x)=\\sqrt{4-x}-\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{4-x}}=\\frac{2(4-x)-x}{2\\sqrt{4-x}}=\\frac{8-3x}{2\\sqrt{4-x}}$.\nSetting $f'(x)=0$ gives $x=\\frac{8}{3}$.\nSign check: $f'>0$ for $x<\\frac{8}{3}$ and $f'<0$ for $x>\\frac{8}{3}$, so $x=\\frac{8}{3}$ is a point of local maxima.\nLocal maximum value: $f\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)=\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}=\\frac{16}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{9}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A 13 m long ladder rests against a vertical wall. Its foot is pulled away from the wall along the ground at 0.5 m/s. How fast is the top of the ladder sliding down when the foot is 5 m from the wall?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{5}{12}$ m/s","$\\frac{5}{24}$ m/s","$\\frac{6}{5}$ m/s","$\\frac{1}{2}$ m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $x^2 + y^2 = 169$, $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 0.5$ m/s and $x = 5$ m. Then $y = \\sqrt{169-25} = 12$ m.\nDifferentiating: $2x\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2y\\frac{dy}{dt} = 0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -\\frac{x}{y}\\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -\\frac{5}{12}(0.5) = -\\frac{5}{24}$ m/s.\nThe negative sign means the height is decreasing, so the top slides down at $\\frac{5}{24}$ m/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match each function in List-I with the behaviour at $x = 0$ given in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $x^4$\n(B) $1 - x^2$\n(C) $x^3$\n(D) $x^2 + 2$\nList-II:\n(P) local maximum, with $f''(0) < 0$\n(Q) local minimum, with $f''(0) > 0$\n(R) $f'(0) = 0$, but neither a local maximum nor a local minimum\n(S) local minimum, but the second derivative test fails","image":"","options":["A-S, B-P, C-R, D-Q","A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S","A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $f' = 4x^3$ and $f'' = 12x^2$, so $f''(0) = 0$ and the test fails. But $x^4 \\ge 0$, so 0 is a local minimum: S.\n(B) $f'' = -2 < 0$, so local maximum: P.\n(C) $f' = 3x^2$ does not change sign, so 0 is a point of inflexion: R.\n(D) $f'' = 2 > 0$, so local minimum: Q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function and point) with List-II (nature of the point).\nList-I:\n(a) $x^3$ at $x = 0$\n(b) $3 + |x|$ at $x = 0$\n(c) $x^3 - 3x + 3$ at $x = -1$\n(d) $3x^4 + 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 12$ at $x = -2$\nList-II:\n(i) Local maximum, with local maximum value 5\n(ii) Local minimum, with local minimum value $-20$\n(iii) Neither local maximum nor local minimum\n(iv) Local minimum at a point where $f'$ does not exist","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $f' = 3x^2$ does not change sign at 0, so 0 is a point of inflexion. Match (iii).\n(b) The slope is $-1$ on the left of 0 and $+1$ on the right, and $f'(0)$ does not exist. So 0 is a local minimum. Match (iv).\n(c) $f'' = 6x$, so $f''(-1) = -6 < 0$, giving a local maximum. $f(-1) = -1 + 3 + 3 = 5$. Match (i).\n(d) $f'(-2) = 0$ and $f''(-2) = 72 > 0$, giving a local minimum. $f(-2) = 48 - 32 - 48 + 12 = -20$. Match (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The absolute maximum value of $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$ on the interval $[0, 2]$ is","image":"","options":["2","-2","0","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$ on $[0, 2]$.\nCritical points: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3 = 3(x-1)(x+1) = 0$ gives $x = 1$ inside the interval ($x = -1$ lies outside).\nValues at the critical point and end points: $f(0) = 0$, $f(1) = 1 - 3 = -2$, $f(2) = 8 - 6 = 2$.\nThe largest of these is $2$, at $x = 2$. Note that $-2$ is the absolute minimum, not the maximum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = \\log|\\cos x|$ is decreasing on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$.\nReason (R): The function $\\cos x$ is decreasing on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R: the derivative of $\\cos x$ is $-\\sin x$, which is negative on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$. So R is true.\nA: for $\\cos x \\neq 0$, $f'(x) = \\frac{-\\sin x}{\\cos x} = -\\tan x$.\nOn $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$, $\\tan x < 0$, so $f'(x) > 0$. Hence f is increasing there, and A is false.\nThe reason is the modulus: $\\cos x$ is negative and decreasing on this interval, so $|\\cos x|$ is increasing.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $M$ and $m$ be the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of $f(x) = 2x^3 - 9x^2 + 12x + 1$ on $[0, 3]$. Then $M - m$ equals","image":"","options":["9","5","4","11"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f'(x) = 6x^2 - 18x + 12 = 6(x-1)(x-2)$, so the critical points are $x = 1$ and $x = 2$.\nValues: $f(0) = 1$, $f(1) = 2 - 9 + 12 + 1 = 6$, $f(2) = 16 - 36 + 24 + 1 = 5$, $f(3) = 54 - 81 + 36 + 1 = 10$.\nSo $M = 10$ (at $x = 3$) and $m = 1$ (at $x = 0$). Both extremes occur at end points, not at the local extrema.\n$M - m = 10 - 1 = 9$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Among right circular cones with a given slant height, the cone of maximum volume has semi-vertical angle $\\tan^{-1}\\sqrt{2}$.\nReason (R): For a cone of given slant height $l$, the volume is maximum when the height is $h=\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{3}}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Using $r^2=l^2-h^2$, the volume is $V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}(l^2-h^2)h$.\n$V'=\\frac{\\pi}{3}(l^2-3h^2)=0$ gives $h=\\frac{l}{\\sqrt3}$, and $V''=-2\\pi h<0$ confirms a maximum.\nA is true. At this height $r=l\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$, so $\\tan\\alpha=\\frac{r}{h}=\\sqrt2$.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $f(x) = x^4$, the second derivative test cannot decide whether $x = 0$ is a point of local maxima or local minima.\nReason (R): $f'(0) = 0$ and $f''(0) = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 4x^3$ and $f''(x) = 12x^2$, so $f'(0) = 0$ and $f''(0) = 0$. R is true.\nBy NCERT, the second derivative test fails exactly when $f'(c) = 0$ and $f''(c) = 0$. So A is true, and R is the reason it fails.\nTo settle the nature of $x = 0$, use the first derivative test: $f'$ changes from negative to positive at 0, so $x = 0$ is actually a local minimum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"An open box is made from a square sheet of side 12 cm by cutting equal squares from each corner and folding up the flaps. For the box to have maximum volume, the side of each square cut off should be","image":"","options":["2 cm","3 cm","4 cm","1.5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the side of the cut square is $x$, so the base side is $12-2x$ and the height is $x$.\nFormula: $V(x)=x(12-2x)^2$.\nDifferentiating: $V'(x)=(12-2x)^2-4x(12-2x)=(12-2x)(12-6x)$.\n$V'=0$ gives $x=6$ (no box is formed) or $x=2$.\n$V''(x)=24x-96$, so $V''(2)=-48<0$, which confirms a maximum.\nSo the side to be cut off is 2 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are decreasing on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$?\n$\\cos x$, $\\cos 2x$, $\\cos 3x$, $\\tan x$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Check the sign of each derivative on $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n(i) $\\cos x$: the derivative is $-\\sin x < 0$, so it is decreasing.\n(ii) $\\cos 2x$: the derivative is $-2\\sin 2x$. Since $2x \\in (0, \\pi)$, $\\sin 2x > 0$, so it is decreasing.\n(iii) $\\cos 3x$: the derivative is $-3\\sin 3x$. Since $3x \\in \\left(0, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $\\sin 3x < 0$ when $3x > \\pi$, so the derivative changes sign. Not decreasing.\n(iv) $\\tan x$: the derivative is $\\sec^2 x > 0$, so it is increasing.\nTherefore exactly 2 functions are decreasing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Rate of change of area of a circle with respect to its radius at $r = 4$ cm\n(B) Rate of change of volume of a sphere with respect to its radius at $r = 2$ cm\n(C) Rate of change of circumference of a circle with respect to its radius\n(D) Rate of change of volume of a cube with respect to its edge at $x = 2$ cm\nList-II\n(i) $2\\pi$\n(ii) $8\\pi$\n(iii) $12$\n(iv) $16\\pi$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-i, B-iv, C-ii, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{d}{dr}(\\pi r^2) = 2\\pi r = 8\\pi$ at $r = 4$, so A-ii.\n(B) $\\frac{d}{dr}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) = 4\\pi r^2 = 16\\pi$ at $r = 2$, so B-iv.\n(C) $\\frac{d}{dr}(2\\pi r) = 2\\pi$, so C-i.\n(D) $\\frac{d}{dx}(x^3) = 3x^2 = 12$ at $x = 2$, so D-iii.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A wire of length 44 cm is cut into two pieces. One piece is bent into a square and the other into a circle. If the combined area enclosed is to be minimum, what is the length of the piece used for the square? (Take $\\pi=3.1416$.)","image":"","options":["24.64 cm","19.36 cm","22.00 cm","30.81 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the square have side $a$ and the circle radius $r$, so $4a+2\\pi r=44$ and $r=\\frac{22-2a}{\\pi}$.\nThe total area is $A=a^2+\\frac{(22-2a)^2}{\\pi}$.\n$A'=2a-\\frac{4(22-2a)}{\\pi}=0$ gives $a=\\frac{44}{\\pi+4}$.\n$A''=2+\\frac{8}{\\pi}>0$, so this is a minimum.\nThe square's piece is $4a=\\frac{176}{\\pi+4}\\approx24.64$ cm.\nOption (2), 19.36 cm, is the circle's piece.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = \\cos x$, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$f$ is increasing on $(0, \\pi)$","$f$ is decreasing on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$","$f$ is increasing on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, and hence increasing on the whole of $(0, 2\\pi)$","$f$ is decreasing on $(0, \\pi)$ and increasing on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, so it is neither increasing nor decreasing on $(0, 2\\pi)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = -\\sin x$.\nOn $(0, \\pi)$, $\\sin x > 0$, so $f'(x) < 0$. Hence f is decreasing there, and (1) fails.\nOn $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, $\\sin x < 0$, so $f'(x) > 0$. Hence f is increasing there, and (2) fails.\nBecause f decreases on one part of $(0, 2\\pi)$ and increases on the other, it is monotonic on neither the whole interval nor its full length. So (3) fails and (4) holds.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For the function $f(x) = x^3$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, which of the following statements about the point $x = 0$ is correct?","image":"","options":["$x = 0$ is a point of local minima because $f'(0) = 0$","$x = 0$ is a critical point, but it is neither a point of local maxima nor a point of local minima","$x = 0$ is not a critical point, because $f$ is increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$","$f'(x)$ changes sign from positive to negative as x increases through 0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 3x^2$, so $f'(0) = 0$. Hence 0 is a critical point, which rules out (3).\n$f'(x) > 0$ on both sides of 0, so $f'$ does not change sign. By the first derivative test, 0 is a point of inflexion, which rules out (4).\nA zero derivative alone does not guarantee an extremum, so (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = x^2 + ax + 3$ is increasing on the interval $[2, 4]$. The least value of a for which this holds is","image":"","options":["$-4$","$-8$","$-2$","$4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x) = x^2 + ax + 3$ on $[2, 4]$.\nFormula: $f'(x) = 2x + a$. We need $f'(x) \\geq 0$ on $[2,4]$, vanishing at most at isolated points.\nSince $2x + a$ increases with x, its smallest value on the interval is at $x = 2$, namely $4 + a$.\nSo we need $4 + a \\geq 0$, i.e. $a \\geq -4$. At $a = -4$, $f' = 2x - 4$ is zero only at the endpoint $x = 2$, so f is still increasing.\nSo the least value is $a = -4$. (Using $x = 4$ instead would wrongly give $-8$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = (x-1)^3 (x+2)^2$. The largest open interval on which f is strictly decreasing is","image":"","options":["$\\left(-2, -\\frac{4}{5}\\right)$","$\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}, 1\\right)$","$(-2, 1)$","$(-\\infty, -2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Product rule: $f'(x) = 3(x-1)^2(x+2)^2 + 2(x-1)^3(x+2)$.\nFactor: $f'(x) = (x-1)^2(x+2)[3(x+2) + 2(x-1)] = (x-1)^2(x+2)(5x+4)$.\nThe critical points are $-2$, $-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $1$. The factor $(x-1)^2 \\geq 0$, so f' does not change sign at $x = 1$.\nSigns: for $x < -2$, f' > 0; for $-2 < x < -\\frac{4}{5}$, f' < 0; for $x > -\\frac{4}{5}$ (except $x = 1$), f' > 0.\nSo f decreases only on $(-2, -\\frac{4}{5})$. Option (3) wrongly treats $x = 1$ as a sign change.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^{\\frac{2}{3}}(x - 5)$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. If $f$ has a point of local maxima at $x = a$ and a point of local minima at $x = b$, then $f(a) + f(b)$ equals","image":"","options":["$-3\\sqrt[3]{2}$","$-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$","$3\\sqrt[3]{4}$","$-6\\sqrt[3]{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $f(x)=x^{5/3}-5x^{2/3}$.\nFormula: $f'(x)=\\frac{5}{3}x^{2/3}-\\frac{10}{3}x^{-1/3}=\\frac{5(x-2)}{3x^{1/3}}$.\nCritical points: $x=2$ (where $f'=0$) and $x=0$ (where $f$ is not differentiable).\nSigns: for $x<0$, $f'>0$; for $0<x<2$, $f'<0$; for $x>2$, $f'>0$. So $a=0$ is a local maxima and $b=2$ is a local minima.\nSubstituting: $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=2^{2/3}(-3)=-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$. So the sum is $-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For $f(x) = x^3$, we have $f'(0) = 0$.\nReason (R): Every point at which the derivative of a function vanishes is a point of local maxima or local minima.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: $f'(x) = 3x^2$, so $f'(0) = 0$. A is true.\nR: The converse of Theorem 2 need not hold. For $x^3$, $f'(x) = 3x^2 \\ge 0$ on both sides of 0, so $f'$ does not change sign.\nHence 0 is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum; it is a point of inflexion. So R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, a real valued function f is said to be increasing at a point $x_0$ of its domain if:","image":"","options":["there exists an open interval I containing $x_0$ such that f is increasing in I","$f(x_0) > f(x)$ for all x in the domain","$f(x_0 + h) > f(x_0)$ for at least one $h > 0$","f is defined at $x_0$ and $f(x_0) > 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Definition 2 of NCERT says f is increasing at $x_0$ if some open interval I containing $x_0$ exists on which f is increasing.\nOption (2) describes a maximum value, not monotonicity.\nOption (3) compares only one point. That cannot ensure increasing behaviour near $x_0$.\nOption (4) is about the sign of f, not its behaviour.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A pebble dropped into a still pond produces circular ripples whose radius grows at 6 cm/s. How fast is the area enclosed by a ripple increasing when its radius is 15 cm?","image":"","options":["$90\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$30\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$180\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$360\\pi$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 6$ cm/s and $r = 15$ cm.\nFormula: $A = \\pi r^2$, so $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt}$ (by the chain rule).\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi(15)(6) = 180\\pi$.\nSo the area increases at $180\\pi$ cm$^2$/s. The value $90\\pi$ comes from forgetting the factor 2 in the derivative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^{100} + \\sin x - 1$.\nStatement I: f is increasing on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right)$.\nStatement II: f is decreasing on $(-1, 0)$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 100x^{99} + \\cos x$.\nStatement I: For $x > \\frac{\\pi}{2} > 1$, $100x^{99} > 100$ while $\\cos x \\ge -1$. So $f'(x) > 0$ and f is increasing. True.\nStatement II: $f'(0) = 0 + 1 = 1 > 0$, so $f'(x) > 0$ near 0. Near $-1$, $f'(x) \\approx -100 + \\cos 1 < 0$.\nThe derivative changes sign on $(-1, 0)$, so f is neither increasing nor decreasing there. False.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = 3x^4 - 8x^3 - 6x^2 + 24x + 7$, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) f is increasing on $(-1, 1)$\n(ii) f is decreasing on $(1, 3)$\n(iii) f is increasing on $(2, \\infty)$\n(iv) f is decreasing on $(-2, -1)$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 12x^3 - 24x^2 - 12x + 24 = 12(x+1)(x-1)(x-2)$.\nThe critical points $-1, 1, 2$ give this sign table: $(-\\infty,-1)$: −; $(-1,1)$: +; $(1,2)$: −; $(2,\\infty)$: +.\n(i) Correct: $f' > 0$ on $(-1,1)$.\n(ii) Incorrect: f decreases on $(1,2)$ but increases on $(2,3)$.\n(iii) Correct: $f' > 0$ for $x > 2$.\n(iv) Correct: $(-2,-1)$ lies inside $(-\\infty,-1)$, where $f' < 0$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Two positive numbers have a sum of 12. What is the least possible value of the sum of their cubes?","image":"","options":["432","288","1728","216"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $S(x)=x^3+(12-x)^3$.\nThen $S'(x)=3x^2-3(12-x)^2=0$, so $x=12-x$ and $x=6$.\n$S''(x)=6x+6(12-x)=72>0$, so $x=6$ gives a minimum.\nSubstituting: $S(6)=6^3+6^3=216+216=432$.\nOption (4), 216, is the cube of only one of the two numbers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A circular metal disc is heated, and its radius increases at 0.02 cm/s. Find the rate at which its area increases when the radius is 5 cm.","image":"","options":["$0.1\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$0.2\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$0.4\\pi$ cm$^2$/s","$0.02\\pi$ cm$^2$/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 0.02$ cm/s and $r = 5$ cm.\nFormula: $A = \\pi r^2$, so $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi(5)(0.02) = 0.2\\pi$.\nSo the area increases at $0.2\\pi$ cm$^2$/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = (x - 3)^4 (x + 2)^3$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$x = -2$ is a point of local minima","$x = \\frac{1}{7}$ is a point of local maxima and $x = 3$ is a point of local minima","$x = 3$ is a point of inflexion","$f$ has exactly three points of local extrema"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 4(x-3)^3(x+2)^3 + 3(x-3)^4(x+2)^2$.\nTaking out the common factor: $f'(x) = (x-3)^3(x+2)^2[4(x+2) + 3(x-3)] = (x-3)^3(x+2)^2(7x - 1)$.\nThe critical points are $x = -2, \\frac{1}{7}, 3$.\nAt $x = -2$: the factor $(x+2)^2$ does not change sign, so $f'$ keeps the same sign. This is a point of inflexion, not a minimum.\nAt $x = \\frac{1}{7}$: $(x-3)^3 < 0$ nearby and $(7x - 1)$ goes from $-$ to $+$, so $f'$ goes from $+$ to $-$. Local maxima.\nAt $x = 3$: $f'$ goes from $-$ to $+$. Local minima.\nSo there are only two extrema.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The rate of change of the circumference of a circle with respect to its radius is the same for a circle of radius 2 cm as for a circle of radius 20 cm.\nReason (R): For $C = 2\\pi r$, $\\frac{dC}{dr} = 2\\pi$, which does not depend on r.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate $C = 2\\pi r$ with respect to r to get $\\frac{dC}{dr} = 2\\pi$.\nR is true, because this derivative is a constant.\nA is true, because a constant rate gives the same value for every radius.\nR directly explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Two positive numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x+y=28$. For which value of $x$ is the product $x^2y^5$ maximum?","image":"","options":["8","20","14","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $P(x)=x^2(28-x)^5$.\n$P'(x)=2x(28-x)^5-5x^2(28-x)^4=x(28-x)^4(56-7x)$.\nSetting $P'(x)=0$ in $0<x<28$ gives $x=8$.\nThe factor $(56-7x)$ is positive for $x<8$ and negative for $x>8$, so $P'$ changes from + to − and $x=8$ is a maximum.\nThen $y=20$. Option (2), 20, is the value of $y$, not $x$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The rate of change of the area of a circle with respect to its radius is numerically equal to its circumference.\nStatement II: If the radius of a circle increases at a constant rate, then its area also increases at a constant rate, whatever the radius.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\frac{dA}{dr} = 2\\pi r$, which is exactly the circumference. So Statement I is true.\nStatement II: by the chain rule, $\\frac{dA}{dt} = 2\\pi r\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nEven when $\\frac{dr}{dt}$ is constant, this rate grows with r, so it is not constant. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let f be continuous on $[a, b]$ and differentiable on $(a, b)$ with $f'(x) = 0$ for each $x \\in (a, b)$. By Theorem 1 of NCERT, f on $[a, b]$ is:","image":"","options":["strictly increasing","strictly decreasing","a constant function","possibly non-constant, since $f'$ may be non-zero at a and b"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Theorem 1(c) states that if $f'(x) = 0$ for each x in $(a, b)$, then f is constant on $[a, b]$.\nVia MVT: for any $x_1 < x_2$ in $[a, b]$, $f(x_2) - f(x_1) = f'(c)(x_2 - x_1) = 0$.\nOnly interior points c are used, and continuity covers the end points. So option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A man of height 1.6 m walks away from a 4.8 m high lamp post at a uniform speed of 6 km/h. At what speed does the tip of his shadow move along the ground?","image":"","options":["3 km/h","6 km/h","9 km/h","12 km/h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: man MN = 1.6 m, lamp post AB = 4.8 m, $\\frac{dl}{dt} = 6$ km/h, where $l$ = AM and $s$ = shadow MS.\nSimilar triangles: $\\frac{s}{l + s} = \\frac{1.6}{4.8} = \\frac{1}{3}$, so $s = \\frac{l}{2}$.\nThen $\\frac{ds}{dt} = 3$ km/h. This is the rate of growth of the shadow's length.\nThe tip is at distance $l + s$ from the post, so its speed is $6 + 3 = 9$ km/h.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For the function $f(x) = |x - 2| + 1$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the point $x = 2$ is","image":"","options":["a critical point at which $f'(2) = 0$","a critical point, because $f$ is not differentiable there, and it is a point of local minima","not a critical point, since $f'(2)$ does not exist","a point of local maxima"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Definition: a critical point is a point where either $f'(c)=0$ or $f$ is not differentiable.\nAt $x=2$ the graph has a corner, so $f'(2)$ does not exist. Hence $x=2$ is a critical point, not one with $f'(2)=0$.\nFor $x<2$, $f'(x)=-1<0$; for $x>2$, $f'(x)=1>0$. The sign changes from $-$ to $+$, so $x=2$ is a point of local minima with value $f(2)=1$.\nOption 3 is tempting, but non-differentiable points are critical points by definition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along the curve $6y = x^3 + 2$. At which points on the curve is the $y$-coordinate changing twice as fast as the $x$-coordinate?","image":"","options":["$(2, \\frac{5}{3})$ only","$(2, \\frac{5}{3})$ and $(-2, \\frac{5}{3})$","$(4, 11)$ and $(-4, -\\frac{31}{3})$","$(2, \\frac{5}{3})$ and $(-2, -1)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Differentiate with respect to $t$: $6\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3x^2\\frac{dx}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nSet the condition $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2\\frac{dx}{dt}$. Then $\\frac{x^2}{2} = 2$, so $x = \\pm 2$.\nFor $x = 2$: $y = \\frac{8+2}{6} = \\frac{5}{3}$.\nFor $x = -2$: $y = \\frac{-8+2}{6} = -1$.\nOption (1) misses the negative root. Option (2) uses the wrong $y$-value at $x = -2$. Option (3) corresponds to the condition '8 times as fast'.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For the function $f(x) = x^3 - 12x + 5$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the local maximum value is","image":"","options":["21","$-11$","5","13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=x^3-12x+5$.\nCritical points: $f'(x)=3x^2-12=3(x-2)(x+2)=0$, so $x=\\pm 2$.\nSecond derivative: $f''(x)=6x$. Since $f''(-2)=-12<0$, $x=-2$ is a point of local maxima. Since $f''(2)=12>0$, $x=2$ is a point of local minima.\nLocal maximum value: $f(-2)=-8+24+5=21$.\nThe value $-11$ is $f(2)$, which is the local minimum value.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = |x|$ with its domain restricted to the interval $[-3, 2]$. What is the maximum value of $f$ on this interval?","image":"","options":["2","3","0","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On R, $|x|$ has a minimum value of 0 at $x = 0$ but no maximum value.\nOn $[-3, 2]$ the graph falls from $x = -3$ to $x = 0$ and then rises up to $x = 2$.\nThe largest value therefore occurs at an end point: $f(-3) = 3$ and $f(2) = 2$.\nSo the maximum value is 3, attained at $x = -3$. Choosing 2 wrongly assumes the maximum must lie at the right end point.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Two positive real numbers have a sum of 20. What is the largest possible value of their product?","image":"","options":["100","96","75","200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the numbers be $x$ and $20-x$. Then $P(x)=x(20-x)=20x-x^2$.\n$P'(x)=20-2x=0$ gives $x=10$.\n$P''(x)=-2<0$, so $x=10$ is a point of maxima.\nThe numbers are 10 and 10, and $P=10\\times10=100$.\nThe value 96 comes from the unequal pair 12 and 8, so it is not the maximum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Maximum value of $\\sin 2x + 5$\n(b) Minimum value of $|\\sin 4x + 3|$\n(c) Minimum value of $|x+2| - 1$\n(d) Maximum value of $-|x+1| + 3$\nList-II\n(i) $-1$\n(ii) $2$\n(iii) $3$\n(iv) $6$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Since $-1\\le\\sin 2x\\le 1$, the maximum of $\\sin 2x+5$ is $1+5=6$, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) $\\sin 4x+3$ lies in $[2,4]$ and is always positive, so the minimum of $|\\sin 4x+3|$ is 2, so (b)-(ii).\n(c) Since $|x+2|\\ge 0$, the minimum of $|x+2|-1$ is $0-1=-1$, at $x=-2$, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Since $-|x+1|\\le 0$, the maximum of $-|x+1|+3$ is 3, at $x=-1$, so (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function on interval) with List-II (absolute maximum value).\nList-I:\n(a) $\\cos^2 x + \\sin x$, $x \\in [0, \\pi]$\n(b) $\\sin x + \\sqrt{3}\\cos x$, $x \\in [0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]$\n(c) $2x^3 - 15x^2 + 36x + 1$, $x \\in [1, 4]$\n(d) $x^{2/3}$, $x \\in [-1, 8]$\nList-II:\n(i) 2\n(ii) 33\n(iii) $\\frac{5}{4}$\n(iv) 4","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $f' = \\cos x(1 - 2\\sin x)$, so the critical points are $\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. $f(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) = \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{5}{4}$; all other checked values equal 1. Maximum $= \\frac{5}{4}$, so (iii).\n(b) $f = 2\\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{3})$. The angle reaches $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Maximum $= 2$, so (i).\n(c) $f(1) = 24$, $f(2) = 29$, $f(3) = 28$, $f(4) = 33$. Maximum $= 33$, so (ii).\n(d) $f$ is not differentiable at 0. $f(-1) = 1$, $f(0) = 0$, $f(8) = 4$. Maximum $= 4$, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $x = 0$ is a critical point of $f(x) = |x|$.\n(ii) For $f(x) = x^3$, since $f'(0) = 0$, the point $x = 0$ is a point of local minima.\n(iii) If $f$ has a local maximum at an interior point $c$, then $f'(c)$ must equal 0.\n(iv) If $f$ is continuous at a critical point $c$ and $f'(x)$ does not change sign as $x$ increases through $c$, then $c$ is a point of inflexion.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $|x|$ is not differentiable at 0, so 0 is a critical point.\n(ii) Incorrect: $f'(x) = 3x^2 \\ge 0$ does not change sign at 0, so $x = 0$ is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum.\n(iii) Incorrect: by Theorem 2, either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f$ is not differentiable at $c$. For example, $-|x|$ has a local maximum at 0 where $f'(0)$ does not exist.\n(iv) Correct: this is part (iii) of the first derivative test.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"What is the instantaneous rate of change of the area of a circle with respect to its radius when the radius is 7 cm?","image":"","options":["$14\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$49\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$7\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$28\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = \\pi r^2$ and r = 7 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{dA}{dr} = 2\\pi r$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dr} = 2\\pi(7) = 14\\pi$.\nThe tempting option $49\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm is the area itself, not its rate of change.\nSo the rate of change is $14\\pi$ cm$^2$ per cm of radius.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A water tank is an inverted right circular cone with its vertex at the bottom and semi-vertical angle $\\tan^{-1}(0.5)$. Water is poured in at $2\\pi$ m$^3$/h. At the instant the water is 3 m deep, at what rate is the area of the circular water surface increasing?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ m$^2$/h","$\\frac{8\\pi}{9}$ m$^2$/h","$\\frac{16\\pi}{9}$ m$^2$/h","$\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ m$^2$/h"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{r}{h} = \\tan\\alpha = 0.5$, so $r = \\frac{h}{2}$.\nVolume: $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{\\pi h^3}{12}$. So $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi h^2}{4}\\frac{dh}{dt}$.\nAt $h = 3$: $2\\pi = \\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\frac{dh}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dh}{dt} = \\frac{8}{9}$ m/h.\nSurface area: $A = \\pi r^2 = \\frac{\\pi h^2}{4}$. So $\\frac{dA}{dt} = \\frac{\\pi h}{2}\\frac{dh}{dt} = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\cdot\\frac{8}{9} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ m$^2$/h.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Water leaks out of a tank so that the volume V of water in it falls by 3 litres every minute. If t is the time in minutes, the value of $\\frac{dV}{dt}$ (in litres/min) is","image":"","options":["$-3$","$3$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A derivative is negative when the quantity decreases as the other variable increases.\nHere V decreases as t increases, so $\\frac{dV}{dt}$ must be negative.\nThe magnitude of the rate is 3 L/min, so $\\frac{dV}{dt} = -3$ L/min.\nThe option 3 gives the correct size but the wrong sign, because it describes an increasing volume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about absolute extreme values is correct?","image":"","options":["Every function that is continuous on an open interval $(a, b)$ attains an absolute maximum value in $(a, b)$.","If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$, then $f$ attains both its absolute maximum value and its absolute minimum value at least once in $[a, b]$.","The absolute maximum value of a function on $[a, b]$ always occurs at a point where $f'(x) = 0$.","If $f$ is differentiable on $[a, b]$ and attains its absolute maximum at the end point $x = a$, then $f'(a)$ must be $0$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) False: $f(x) = x$ is continuous on $(0, 1)$, but it has no maximum value there.\n(ii) True: this is Theorem 5. A continuous function on a closed interval attains its absolute maximum and minimum at least once.\n(iii) False: the extreme value can occur at an end point. For $f(x) = x + 2$ on $[0, 1]$ the maximum is at $x = 1$, where $f' \\neq 0$.\n(iv) False: Theorem 6 gives $f'(c) = 0$ only at interior points. For $f(x) = -x$ on $[0, 1]$ the maximum is at $x = 0$, but $f'(0) = -1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are increasing on the whole of $\\mathbb{R}$?\n(i) $e^{x}$\n(ii) $7x - 3$\n(iii) $x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x$\n(iv) $x^2$\n(v) $\\cos x$\n(vi) $x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 100$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Increasing: $f' = e^x > 0$ for all $x$.\n(ii) Increasing: $f' = 7 > 0$.\n(iii) Increasing: $f' = 3x^2 - 6x + 4 = 3(x-1)^2 + 1 > 0$.\n(iv) Not increasing: $f' = 2x < 0$ for $x < 0$, so $x^2$ decreases there.\n(v) Not increasing: $\\cos x$ decreases on $(0, \\pi)$.\n(vi) Increasing: $f' = 3(x-1)^2 \\ge 0$, and it is zero only at the single point $x = 1$.\nSo four functions, (i), (ii), (iii) and (vi), are increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For which set of real values of k is the function $f(x) = x^3 + kx^2 + 3x + 5$ increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$?","image":"","options":["$[-3, 3]$","$(-3, 3)$","$[-9, 9]$","$(-\\infty, -3] \\cup [3, \\infty)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = 3x^2 + 2kx + 3$. For f to be increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$, we need $f'(x) \\ge 0$ for all x, with equality at most at isolated points.\nAn upward-opening quadratic is non-negative everywhere when its discriminant is $\\le 0$: $4k^2 - 36 \\le 0$, so $|k| \\le 3$.\nAt $k = \\pm 3$: $f'(x) = 3(x \\pm 1)^2$, which is zero only at one point. This is like $x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 100$, which NCERT shows is increasing. So the end points are included.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A point P lies on the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle. Its perpendicular distances from the two legs are 1 cm and 8 cm. The minimum possible length of the hypotenuse is","image":"","options":["$5\\sqrt{5}$ cm","9 cm","$\\sqrt{65}$ cm","$6\\sqrt{3}$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the hypotenuse make angle $\\theta$ with one leg. Splitting it at P gives $L(\\theta)=a\\sec\\theta+b\\,\\mathrm{cosec}\\,\\theta$, with $a=1$ and $b=8$.\nDifferentiating: $L'=a\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta-b\\,\\mathrm{cosec}\\,\\theta\\cot\\theta=0$ gives $\\tan^3\\theta=\\frac{b}{a}=8$, so $\\tan\\theta=2$.\n$L'$ changes from − to +, so this is a minimum.\nThen $\\sec\\theta=\\sqrt5$ and $\\mathrm{cosec}\\,\\theta=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}$.\nSubstituting: $L=\\sqrt5+4\\sqrt5=5\\sqrt5$, which matches $(a^{2/3}+b^{2/3})^{3/2}=(1+4)^{3/2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The function $f(x) = x$, $x \\in (0, 1)$, has neither a maximum value nor a minimum value.\nStatement II: Every continuous function on a closed interval has a maximum value and a minimum value.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. For any $x_0$ chosen near 0, the point $\\frac{x_0}{2}$ gives a smaller value. For any $x_1$ chosen near 1, the point $\\frac{x_1 + 1}{2}$ gives a larger value. So no extreme value is attained on the open interval.\nStatement II is true. This is the general result stated in NCERT (Theorem 5).\nOn $[0, 1]$, the same function attains a minimum of 0 and a maximum of 1. The open interval is exactly why Statement I holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"If $x = t^2$ and $y = t^3$, then the value of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at t = 2 is","image":"","options":["3","12","4","$\\frac{1}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Find each rate with respect to t: $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2$.\nApply the chain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3t^2}{2t} = \\frac{3t}{2}$, which is valid because $2t \\neq 0$ at t = 2.\nAt t = 2, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3$.\nThe option 12 is only $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ at t = 2, so it is not the required ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many critical points does the function $f(x) = |x^2 - 4|$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, have?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Critical points are points where $f'=0$ or where $f$ is not differentiable.\nFor $|x|>2$, $f(x)=x^2-4$ and $f'(x)=2x$, which is never 0 in this region.\nFor $|x|<2$, $f(x)=4-x^2$ and $f'(x)=-2x$, so $f'(0)=0$.\nAt $x=\\pm2$, the left and right derivatives are $\\mp4$ and $\\pm4$. These do not match, so $f$ is not differentiable at $x=\\pm2$.\nThe critical points are $-2, 0, 2$, which gives 3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are increasing throughout the stated domain?\n(i) $e^{2x}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$\n(ii) $\\log x$ on $(0, \\infty)$\n(iii) $7x - 3$ on $\\mathbb{R}$\n(iv) $\\cos x$ on $(0, 2\\pi)$\n(v) $x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x$ on $\\mathbb{R}$\n(vi) $10 - 6x$ on $\\mathbb{R}$\n(vii) $x^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: $f' = 2e^{2x} > 0$.\n(ii) Yes: $f' = \\frac{1}{x} > 0$ for $x > 0$.\n(iii) Yes: $f' = 7 > 0$.\n(iv) No: it decreases on $(0, \\pi)$ and increases on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$.\n(v) Yes: $f' = 3(x-1)^2 + 1 > 0$.\n(vi) No: $f' = -6 < 0$. (vii) No: it decreases for $x < 0$.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = 3x^4 + 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 12$, let $M$ be the local maximum value and $m$ the smaller of the two local minimum values. Then $M - m$ equals","image":"","options":["32","19","5","27"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First derivative: $f'(x)=12x^3+12x^2-24x=12x(x-1)(x+2)$, so the critical points are $x=0,1,-2$.\nSecond derivative: $f''(x)=36x^2+24x-24$.\n$f''(0)=-24<0$ gives a local maximum, $M=f(0)=12$.\n$f''(1)=36>0$ gives a local minimum, $f(1)=3+4-12+12=7$.\n$f''(-2)=72>0$ gives a local minimum, $f(-2)=48-32-48+12=-20$.\nThe smaller local minimum is $m=-20$, so $M-m=12-(-20)=32$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The radius $r$ of a circular metal plate is a variable. What is the rate of change of the area of the plate with respect to its radius at the instant when $r = 7$ cm?","image":"","options":["$14\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$49\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$7\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm","$28\\pi$ cm$^2$/cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: area $A = \\pi r^2$ and $r = 7$ cm.\nFormula: the rate of change of $A$ with respect to $r$ is $\\frac{dA}{dr} = 2\\pi r$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dA}{dr} = 2\\pi(7) = 14\\pi$.\nOption (2), $49\\pi$, is the area $\\pi r^2$ itself, not its rate of change.\nOption (4), $28\\pi$, comes from wrongly doubling $2\\pi r$.\nSo the area changes at $14\\pi$ cm$^2$ per cm of radius.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"An open-top box is made from a rectangular sheet of cardboard 16 cm by 10 cm. Equal squares of side $x$ cm are cut from each corner and the flaps are folded up. What is the maximum possible volume of the box?","image":"","options":["112 $cm^3$","120 $cm^3$","144 $cm^3$","160 $cm^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: length $= 16-2x$, breadth $= 10-2x$, height $= x$, with $0<x<5$.\nVolume: $V = x(16-2x)(10-2x) = 4x^3-52x^2+160x$.\nDerivative: $V' = 12x^2-104x+160 = 4(3x-20)(x-2)$, so $V'=0$ at $x=2$ or $x=\\frac{20}{3}$. Reject $x=\\frac{20}{3}$ because it exceeds 5 and would make the breadth negative.\nSecond derivative test: $V''=24x-104$, so $V''(2)=-56<0$. Hence $x=2$ gives a maximum.\nSubstituting: $V(2)=2\\times12\\times6=144$ $cm^3$. The tempting 120 comes from $x=3$, which is not the critical point.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^{4/3} - 4x^{1/3}$, $x \\in [-1, 8]$. If $M$ and $m$ denote the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of $f$ on this interval, then $M - m$ equals","image":"","options":["11","8","13","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$f'(x) = \\frac{4}{3}x^{1/3} - \\frac{4}{3}x^{-2/3} = \\frac{4(x-1)}{3x^{2/3}}$.\nSo $f'(x) = 0$ at $x = 1$, and $f'$ is not defined at $x = 0$. Both are critical points.\nValues: $f(-1) = 1 + 4 = 5$, $f(0) = 0$, $f(1) = 1 - 4 = -3$, $f(8) = 16 - 8 = 8$.\nSo $M = 8$ and $m = -3$, giving $M - m = 11$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = x^{\\frac{2}{3}}(x - 5)$, $x \\in$ R. Which of the following correctly describes the local extrema of $f$?","image":"","options":["Local maximum at $x = 0$ with value 0, and local minimum at $x = 2$ with value $-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$","The only critical point is $x = 2$, which gives a local minimum value $-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$; there is no local maximum","Local maximum at $x = 2$ with value $-3\\sqrt[3]{4}$, and local minimum at $x = 0$ with value 0","$x = 0$ is a point of inflexion, and there is a local minimum at $x = 2$ with value $-3\\sqrt[3]{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $f = x^{5/3} - 5x^{2/3}$. Then $f'(x) = \\frac{5}{3}x^{2/3} - \\frac{10}{3}x^{-1/3} = \\frac{5(x-2)}{3x^{1/3}}$.\nThe critical points are $x = 2$, where $f' = 0$, and $x = 0$, where $f$ is not differentiable. Missing $x = 0$ leads to option (2).\nSign of $f'$: positive for $x < 0$, negative on $(0, 2)$, positive for $x > 2$.\nSo $x = 0$ is a local maximum with $f(0) = 0$, and $x = 2$ is a local minimum with $f(2) = 2^{2/3}(-3) = -3\\sqrt[3]{4}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^2$ be defined only on the closed interval $[-3, 2]$. What is the maximum value of $f$ on this interval?","image":"","options":["4","9","13","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f(x)=x^2$ on $[-3,2]$. On all of R this function has no maximum, but restricting the domain changes that.\nFormula: $f'(x)=2x$, so the only critical point is $x=0$. Compare $f$ there and at both end points.\nSubstituting: $f(0)=0$, $f(2)=4$, $f(-3)=9$.\nSo the maximum value is 9, attained at $x=-3$, and the minimum value is 0. The value 4 is wrong because it ignores the left end point.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The length of a rectangle decreases at 4 cm/min while its width increases at 4 cm/min.\nAssertion (A): The perimeter of the rectangle stays constant during this change.\nReason (R): For a rectangle of length $x$ and width $y$, $\\frac{dP}{dt} = 2\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $P = 2(x+y)$, so $\\frac{dP}{dt} = 2\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}+\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)$. R is true.\nA: Substituting, $\\frac{dP}{dt} = 2(-4+4) = 0$. The perimeter does not change, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = 2x^3 - 9x^2 + 12x$ on the closed interval $[0, 3]$, find (absolute maximum value) $-$ (local maximum value).","image":"","options":["4","0","5","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 6x^2 - 18x + 12 = 6(x-1)(x-2)$, so $f' = 0$ at $x = 1$ and $x = 2$.\nAt $x = 1$, $f'$ changes from + to −, so $x = 1$ is a local maximum with $f(1) = 5$. ($x = 2$ is a local minimum with $f(2) = 4$.)\nFor the absolute maximum, also check the end points: $f(0) = 0$ and $f(3) = 54 - 81 + 36 = 9$.\nThe absolute maximum is 9, attained at the end point $x = 3$, so it differs from the local maximum.\nRequired difference: $9 - 5 = 4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function and point) with List-II (nature of the point).\nList-I\n(A) $f(x) = x^3$ at $x = 0$\n(B) $f(x) = 3 + |x|$ at $x = 0$\n(C) $f(x) = x^4$ at $x = 0$\n(D) $f(x) = \\cos x$ at $x = 0$\nList-II\n(i) Local maximum; second derivative test is conclusive\n(ii) Local minimum; function not differentiable there\n(iii) Point of inflexion\n(iv) Local minimum, although the second derivative test fails","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) $f' = 3x^2$ does not change sign at 0, so it is a point of inflexion: A-iii.\n(B) $|x|$ has a corner at 0; $f'$ goes from $-1$ to $+1$, giving a local minimum where $f$ is not differentiable: B-ii.\n(C) $f' = 4x^3$ and $f'' = 12x^2 = 0$ at 0, so the second derivative test fails. But $f'$ goes from negative to positive, so it is a local minimum: C-iv.\n(D) $f'(0) = -\\sin 0 = 0$ and $f''(0) = -\\cos 0 = -1 < 0$, a local maximum: D-i.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$, $x \\in [0, 2]$.\nAssertion (A): The absolute maximum of $f$ on $[0, 2]$ occurs at $x = 1$.\nReason (R): If a differentiable function attains its absolute maximum or minimum at an interior point $c$ of a closed interval, then $f'(c) = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3 = 0$ gives $x = 1$ in $[0, 2]$.\nEvaluate $f$ at the critical point and the end points: $f(0) = 0$, $f(1) = -2$, $f(2) = 8 - 6 = 2$.\nThe absolute maximum is $2$ at $x = 2$, and the absolute minimum is $-2$ at $x = 1$. So A is false.\nR is true; this is NCERT Theorem 6.\nR also explains why $x = 1$ is a candidate point, but it does not tell us whether that point gives the maximum or the minimum.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The absolute maximum value of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3}\\sin x + \\cos x$ on $\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ is","image":"","options":["2","$\\sqrt{3}$","$1+\\sqrt{3}$","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $f'(x) = \\sqrt{3}\\cos x - \\sin x$.\nSetting $f'(x) = 0$ gives $\\tan x = \\sqrt{3}$, so $x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nValues: $f(0) = 1$, $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 2$, $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sqrt{3}$.\nThe greatest value is $2$. The option $1 + \\sqrt{3}$ wrongly adds the separate maxima of the two terms, which do not occur at the same $x$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The set of all real values of a for which $f(x) = x^2 + ax + 3$ is increasing on $[2, 5]$ is","image":"","options":["$a \\geq -4$","$a \\geq -10$","$a \\leq -4$","$a > 4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiate: $f'(x) = 2x + a$, which grows with x. Its least value on the interval is near $x = 2$.\nWe need $f'(x) > 0$ for every $x \\in (2, 5)$. This holds when $2(2) + a \\geq 0$, i.e. $a \\geq -4$.\nAt $a = -4$, $f'(x) = 2x - 4 > 0$ on $(2, 5)$, and f is continuous on $[2, 5]$. So f is still increasing by Theorem 1.\nOption $a \\geq -10$ wrongly uses the right end $x = 5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The profit (in thousand rupees) of a firm selling $x$ hundred units is $P(x)=60+48x-4x^2$. What is the maximum profit (in thousand rupees)?","image":"","options":["204","60","348","144"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P(x)=60+48x-4x^2$.\nThen $P'(x)=48-8x=0$, which gives $x=6$.\n$P''(x)=-8<0$, so $x=6$ is a point of maxima.\nSubstituting: $P(6)=60+288-144=204$.\nSo the maximum profit is 204 thousand rupees.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $x > 0$, consider $g(x) = \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{3}{x}$. Using the second derivative test, what is the local minimum value of $g$?","image":"","options":["2","3","6","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$g'(x) = \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{3}{x^2}$. Setting $g'(x) = 0$ gives $x^2 = 9$, so $x = 3$ (since $x > 0$).\n$g''(x) = \\frac{6}{x^3}$, so $g''(3) = \\frac{6}{27} = \\frac{2}{9} > 0$. Hence $x = 3$ is a point of local minima.\n$g(3) = \\frac{3}{3} + \\frac{3}{3} = 1 + 1 = 2$.\nThe tempting value 3 is the point $x$ at which the minimum occurs, not the minimum value.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match the functions in List-I with the nature of the point $x = 0$ in List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) $f(x) = x|x|$\n(Q) $f(x) = 1 - x^{\\frac{2}{3}}$\n(R) $f(x) = x^4$\n(S) $f(x) = |x|$\nList-II:\n(1) Local maxima; $f$ not differentiable at 0\n(2) Local minima; $f$ not differentiable at 0\n(3) Local minima; $f'(0) = 0$\n(4) Neither maxima nor minima; $f'(0) = 0$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(P) $f'(x)=2|x|\\ge0$, so $f'(0)=0$ but there is no sign change. It is neither, so it matches (4).\n(Q) $f'(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}x^{-1/3}$ goes from $+$ to $-$ through 0 and is undefined at 0. It is a local max, so it matches (1).\n(R) $f'(x)=4x^3$ goes from $-$ to $+$ and $f'(0)=0$. It is a local min, so it matches (3).\n(S) $|x|$ has a corner at 0 with $f'$ going from $-1$ to $+1$. It is a local min, so it matches (2).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of all rectangles inscribed in a circle of radius 5 cm, the greatest area is 50 $cm^2$.\nStatement II: Of all closed right circular cylindrical cans of a fixed volume, the can with the least total surface area has its height equal to the diameter of its base.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The diagonal of the rectangle is the diameter, 10 cm. Taking the sides as $10\\cos\\theta$ and $10\\sin\\theta$ gives $A=50\\sin2\\theta$. This is greatest (50) when $\\theta=45^\\circ$, i.e. for a square.\nStatement II is correct. Write $S=2\\pi r^2+\\frac{2V}{r}$. Then $S'=4\\pi r-\\frac{2V}{r^2}=0$ gives $V=2\\pi r^3$. Since $V=\\pi r^2h$, this means $h=2r$. Also $S''>0$, so this is a minimum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are increasing on the interval $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$?\n$\\sin x$, $\\cos x$, $\\tan x$, $x^3$, $\\cos 2x$, $e^{2x}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Check the sign of the derivative on $(0, \\pi/2)$:\n$\\sin x$: $\\cos x > 0$, increasing.\n$\\cos x$: $-\\sin x < 0$, decreasing.\n$\\tan x$: $\\sec^2 x > 0$, increasing.\n$x^3$: $3x^2 > 0$, increasing.\n$\\cos 2x$: $-2\\sin 2x < 0$, since $2x \\in (0, \\pi)$; decreasing.\n$e^{2x}$: $2e^{2x} > 0$, increasing.\nSo 4 of the functions are increasing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A stone dropped into a still pond produces circular ripples whose radius increases at 3 cm/s. At the instant the radius is 7 cm, at what rate is the circumference of the ripple increasing?","image":"","options":["$6\\pi$ cm/s","$42\\pi$ cm/s","$14\\pi$ cm/s","$3\\pi$ cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dr}{dt} = 3$ cm/s and $r = 7$ cm.\nFormula: $C = 2\\pi r$, so $\\frac{dC}{dt} = 2\\pi\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dC}{dt} = 2\\pi(3) = 6\\pi$ cm/s.\nThe radius 7 cm is not needed, because the rate of the circumference does not depend on $r$. The option $42\\pi$ wrongly multiplies by $r$, as if this were the area rate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = x$ defined on the open interval $(0, 1)$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$f$ has minimum value 0 and maximum value 1","$f$ has a minimum value at the point closest to 0 but no maximum value","$f$ has neither a maximum value nor a minimum value in $(0,1)$","$f$ has maximum value 1 but no minimum value"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: 0 and 1 are not in the domain, so $f$ never takes the values 0 or 1.\nFor any $x_0\\in(0,1)$, the point $\\frac{x_0}{2}$ is also in $(0,1)$ and gives a smaller value. So no least value exists.\nSimilarly, $\\frac{x_1+1}{2}$ is in $(0,1)$ and gives a larger value than $x_1$. So no greatest value exists.\nThere is no 'point closest to 0', so the second option fails.\nIf the domain were $[0,1]$, the extrema 0 and 1 would occur at the end points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Consider $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 6$.\nStatement I: $f$ is decreasing on $(-\\infty, 2)$ and increasing on $(2, \\infty)$.\nStatement II: The absolute maximum value of $f$ on $[0, 3]$ is 3.\n\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"I: $f'(x) = 2x - 4$. This is negative for $x < 2$ and positive for $x > 2$, so I is true.\nII: The only critical point in $[0, 3]$ is $x = 2$.\nValues: $f(0) = 6$, $f(2) = 2$, $f(3) = 3$.\nSo the absolute maximum is 6 (at $x = 0$), not 3. Here 3 is just the value at the right end. II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"An open-topped box is made from a rectangular sheet $16$ cm by $6$ cm by cutting equal squares of side $x$ cm from each corner and folding up the flaps. The maximum possible volume of the box is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3200}{27}$ cm$^3$","$\\frac{1600}{27}$ cm$^3$","$\\frac{1600}{9}$ cm$^3$","$\\frac{512}{27}$ cm$^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the box has length $16 - 2x$, breadth $6 - 2x$ and height $x$, with $0 < x < 3$.\nVolume: $V = x(16-2x)(6-2x) = 4x^3 - 44x^2 + 96x$.\nDifferentiate: $V' = 12x^2 - 88x + 96 = 4(3x-4)(x-6) = 0$, so $x = \\frac{4}{3}$. We reject $x = 6$ because it exceeds 3.\nCheck: $V'' = 24x - 88 = 32 - 88 = -56 < 0$ at $x = \\frac{4}{3}$, so this is a maximum.\nSubstituting: $V = \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{40}{3} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} = \\frac{1600}{27}$ cm$^3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along the curve $6y = x^3 + 2$. At which points on the curve is its y-coordinate changing twice as fast as its x-coordinate?","image":"","options":["$\\left(2, \\frac{5}{3}\\right)$ only","$(2, -1)$ and $\\left(-2, \\frac{5}{3}\\right)$","$\\left(2, \\frac{5}{3}\\right)$ and $(-2, -1)$","$\\left(4, 11\\right)$ and $\\left(-4, -\\frac{31}{3}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Differentiate with respect to $t$: $6\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3x^2\\frac{dx}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}$.\nThe condition $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2\\frac{dx}{dt}$ gives $\\frac{x^2}{2} = 2$, so $x = \\pm 2$.\nFor $x = 2$: $y = \\frac{8 + 2}{6} = \\frac{5}{3}$. For $x = -2$: $y = \\frac{-8 + 2}{6} = -1$.\nBoth points qualify, so 'only' is wrong, and the swapped y-values do not lie on the curve.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":156,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"For $f(x) = x + 2\\cos x$ on $[0, \\pi]$, the difference between the absolute maximum value and the absolute minimum value of $f$ is","image":"","options":["$2\\sqrt{3} - \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$","$\\sqrt{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{6} + 2$","$2\\sqrt{3}$","$2\\sqrt{3} + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Find critical points: $f'(x) = 1 - 2\\sin x = 0$ gives $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}$, so $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$.\nEvaluate at critical points and end points:\n$f(0) = 2$\n$f(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\sqrt{3} \\approx 2.26$\n$f(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) = \\frac{5\\pi}{6} - \\sqrt{3} \\approx 0.89$\n$f(\\pi) = \\pi - 2 \\approx 1.14$\nSo the maximum is $\\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\sqrt{3}$ and the minimum is $\\frac{5\\pi}{6} - \\sqrt{3}$.\nDifference $= 2\\sqrt{3} - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.37$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":157,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"On which of the following intervals is the function $f(x) = \\sin x$ decreasing?","image":"","options":["$(0, \\pi)$","$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$","$\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$","$(\\pi, 2\\pi)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Here $f'(x) = \\cos x$. The function f is decreasing wherever $f'(x) < 0$.\n$\\cos x < 0$ for every x in $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$, so f is decreasing there.\nOn $(0, \\pi)$, $\\cos x$ is positive up to $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and negative after it, so f is not decreasing on the whole interval.\nOn $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, $\\cos x$ becomes positive after $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, so this option also fails. On $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, f is increasing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":158,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $M$ and $m$ be the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of $f(x) = x^{2/3}(x - 5)$ on the interval $[-1, 8]$. Then $M + m$ equals","image":"","options":["$12 - 3\\sqrt[3]{4}$","6","12","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $f(x) = x^{5/3} - 5x^{2/3}$.\nThen $f'(x) = \\frac{5}{3}x^{2/3} - \\frac{10}{3}x^{-1/3} = \\frac{5(x-2)}{3x^{1/3}}$.\nCritical points: $x = 2$ (where $f' = 0$) and $x = 0$ (where $f$ is not differentiable).\nValues at critical points and end points: $f(-1) = 1 \\cdot (-6) = -6$, $f(0) = 0$, $f(2) = -3\\sqrt[3]{4} \\approx -4.76$, $f(8) = 4 \\cdot 3 = 12$.\nSo $M = 12$ and $m = -6$ (attained at an end point, not at $x = 2$). Hence $M + m = 6$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":159,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions are increasing on the whole of $\\mathbb{R}$?\n(i) $e^{2x}$\n(ii) $7x - 3$\n(iii) $x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x$\n(iv) $\\cos x$\n(v) $x^2 - 4x + 6$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) $f' = 2e^{2x} > 0$ for all $x$: increasing.\n(ii) $f' = 7 > 0$: increasing.\n(iii) $f' = 3x^2 - 6x + 4 = 3(x-1)^2 + 1 > 0$: increasing.\n(iv) $f' = -\\sin x$ changes sign (negative on $(0, \\pi)$, positive on $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$): not increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\n(v) $f' = 2x - 4 < 0$ for $x < 2$: not increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nCount = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":160,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (optimization situation) with List-II (result).\nList-I\n(a) Semi-vertical angle of a cone of given slant height and maximum volume\n(b) Semi-vertical angle of a cone of given total surface area and maximum volume\n(c) Altitude of the cone of maximum volume inscribed in a sphere of radius $r$\n(d) Height of the cylinder of maximum volume inscribed in a cone of height $h$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{h}{3}$\n(ii) $\\frac{4r}{3}$\n(iii) $\\tan^{-1}\\sqrt{2}$\n(iv) $\\sin^{-1}\\frac{1}{3}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) With slant height $l$ fixed, $V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}l^3\\sin^2\\theta\\cos\\theta$. This is greatest when $\\tan\\theta=\\sqrt2$, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) With total surface area fixed, maximum volume occurs when $\\sin\\theta=\\frac13$, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) For the largest cone in a sphere of radius $r$, the altitude is $\\frac{4r}{3}$ and the volume is $\\frac{8}{27}$ of the sphere, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) For the largest cylinder in a cone of height $h$, the cylinder's height is $\\frac{h}{3}$, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":161,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(x) = \\frac{\\log x}{x}$, $x > 0$, attains its maximum at $x = e$. Using this fact, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$e^{\\pi} > \\pi^{e}$","$\\pi^{e} > e^{\\pi}$","$e^{\\pi} = \\pi^{e}$","The two numbers cannot be compared using $f$, since $\\pi$ is not a critical point of $f$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Locating the maximum: $f'(x)=\\frac{1-\\log x}{x^2}$, which is zero at $x=e$. It is $>0$ for $x<e$ and $<0$ for $x>e$, so $x=e$ gives the maximum $\\frac{1}{e}$.\nSince $\\pi\\ne e$, $f(\\pi)<f(e)$, that is, $\\frac{\\log\\pi}{\\pi}<\\frac{1}{e}$.\nMultiplying by $e\\pi>0$: $e\\log\\pi<\\pi$, i.e. $\\log(\\pi^{e})<\\log(e^{\\pi})$.\nSince $\\log$ is increasing, $\\pi^{e}<e^{\\pi}$.\nOption (4) fails because comparing values does not require $\\pi$ to be a critical point.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":162,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"A man 1.6 m tall walks away from the foot of an 8 m high lamp-post at 4 km/h. Let A be the foot of the post, M the man's position, and S the tip of his shadow. Let $AM = l$ and $MS = s$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) Rate at which the shadow lengthens\n(Q) Speed of the tip of the shadow\n(R) Ratio $\\frac{AS}{MS}$\n(S) Ratio $\\frac{l}{s}$\nList-II:\n(i) 1 km/h\n(ii) 5 km/h\n(iii) 5\n(iv) 4","image":"","options":["P-ii, Q-i, R-iv, S-iii","P-i, Q-ii, R-iv, S-iii","P-i, Q-ii, R-iii, S-iv","P-ii, Q-i, R-iii, S-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Similar triangles give $\\frac{MS}{AS} = \\frac{1.6}{8} = \\frac{1}{5}$, so $AS = 5s$. Hence R-iii.\n$l = AS - s = 4s$, so $\\frac{l}{s} = 4$. Hence S-iv.\nFrom $l = 4s$: $\\frac{ds}{dt} = \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{dl}{dt} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1$ km/h. Hence P-i.\nThe tip is at distance $AS = l + s$ from the post, so its speed is $4 + 1 = 5$ km/h. Hence Q-ii.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":163,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $f(x) = x$, $x \\in (0, 1)$, has neither a maximum value nor a minimum value.\nReason (R): Every continuous function on a closed interval has a maximum and a minimum value.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. For any $x_0$ in $(0,1)$, the point $\\frac{x_0}{2}$ gives a smaller value and $\\frac{x_0+1}{2}$ gives a larger value. So no least or greatest value exists.\nR is true. NCERT states that every continuous function on a closed interval attains a maximum and a minimum.\nHowever, $(0,1)$ is an open interval, so R does not explain A. A holds because the end points are excluded.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":164,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"Let $y = \\log(1 + x) - \\frac{2x}{2 + x}$, $x > -1$.\nAssertion (A): y is an increasing function of x throughout its domain.\nReason (R): $\\frac{dy}{dx} > 0$ for every $x > -1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First find the derivative: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{1 + x} - \\frac{2(2 + x) - 2x}{(2 + x)^2} = \\frac{1}{1 + x} - \\frac{4}{(2 + x)^2}$.\nCombining the fractions: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{(2 + x)^2 - 4(1 + x)}{(1 + x)(2 + x)^2} = \\frac{x^2}{(1 + x)(2 + x)^2}$.\nFor $x > -1$ this is $\\ge 0$, and it equals 0 only at the isolated point $x = 0$. So y is still increasing on $(-1, \\infty)$, and A is true.\nR claims the derivative is strictly positive everywhere, but $y'(0) = 0$. So R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-AOD-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Application of Derivatives","qno":165,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Application of Derivatives","type":"mcq","question":"In proving Theorem 1(a) (f continuous on $[a, b]$, differentiable on $(a, b)$, $f'(x) > 0$ on $(a, b)$ implies f increasing on $[a, b]$), we take $x_1 < x_2$ in $[a, b]$ and apply the Mean Value Theorem: $f(x_2) - f(x_1) = f'(c)(x_2 - x_1)$.\nStatement I: Since c lies strictly between $x_1$ and $x_2$, c is in $(a, b)$, so $f'(c) > 0$ and hence $f(x_2) > f(x_1)$.\nStatement II: The proof also needs $f'(a) > 0$ and $f'(b) > 0$, because $x_1$ or $x_2$ may be an end point.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. MVT gives c with $x_1 < c < x_2$, so $a < c < b$ and $f'(c) > 0$. Also $x_2 - x_1 > 0$, so the product is positive.\nStatement II is false. The derivative is used only at the interior point c. At the end points, only continuity is needed, which MVT already requires.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 6 · Application of Derivatives","difficulty":"medium"}]});
